  model contributes is what an operation DRAWS and what it COMMITS ELSEWHERE. See Operation.
-->
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
           xmlns:pm="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0"
           targetNamespace="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0"
           elementFormDefault="qualified"
           version="0.3.0">

  <!-- ==================================================================
       REMAINDER: the object this model contributes.

       ⛔ NOT the buffer. The buffer set is Hopp and Spearman's, closed at three
       in Factory Physics, and this model CONCEDES it rather than extending it,
       so `absorber` below is a BORROWED term naming their taxonomy, exactly like
       a borrowed measurement basis.

       ⭐ What is contributed is the REMAINDER ITSELF: quantized supply meets
       continuous demand, the difference is conserved, and it lands on somebody.
       Management chooses which absorber holds it and whether it is visible; never
       whether it exists.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Remainder">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐ THE NAME IS DELIBERATE AND THE ARITHMETIC SENSE IS APT, NOT A COLLISION:
      a remainder is what is left after dividing into WHOLE UNITS, which is the
      quantized-supply story exactly. Capacity that can only be bought in whole
      units (a person, a reserved block) cannot meet a continuous demand exactly,
      and the difference is this object.

      `absorber` is BORROWED. The expected taxonomy is Factory Physics' closed set
      (inventory · capacity · time). This schema does not restate those three,
      because a closed set restated in a foreign namespace is a fork.

      ⭐ IT IS WRAPPED, BECAUSE A REMAINDER MAY GENUINELY HAVE BEEN ABSORBED BY
      NOTHING. A shop at capacity that turns people away with no waiting list has a
      real remainder — demand exceeded the nameplate — held as `unrealised`, and
      nothing queued, stretched or waited. `absent reason="none"` is the honest
      filing, and it is Factory Physics' own logic rather than an argument against
      it: variability that is not buffered by one of the three is variability whose
      demand went unserved.

      ⚠️ IT STAYS AT THE REMAINDER, ONE PER LAYER, AND THE PLACEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING.
      Moving it beside each `share` was considered and rejected. A rollup across
      several filings — three entities of one group, or two companies in a merger —
      asks which buffers a business leans on, and per-holder that question must be
      weighted by shares. ⛔ The share on a labour layer is `unmeasured` BY DESIGN
      AND PERMANENTLY, so the weighted form is incomputable at exactly the layer this
      model exists for, while the layer-level form answers cleanly. See also
      Nameplate/capacitySlack: how much a supply CAN take and what actually absorbed
      are a pair, read at the same altitude, exactly as `nameplate` and `jagged` are.

      `holder` is CONTRIBUTED: who bears it. See Holder.

      ⚠️ ABSORPTION AND BEARING ARE SEPARATE AXES AND MERGING THEM IS THE COMMONEST
      ERROR HERE. "What soaked it up" and "who ended up carrying it" are different
      questions with different answers, and a model that answers only one of them
      renders a borne cost and an absorbed one identically.

      ⭐⭐ THIS IS ALSO WHERE THE FOURTH-BUFFER ARGUMENT LIVES, AND IT IS ON THE
      HOLDER AXIS RATHER THAN THE ABSORBER AXIS. People are not a fourth buffer
      added to a closed set of three; they are a FIFTH HOLDER of the same three.
      Confusing the two reads as an attack on Factory Physics, which this is not.

      `sign` is normally DERIVED (nameplate minus demand) and is carried anyway
      because the DIRECTION is frequently known when the MAGNITUDE is not. "We ran
      hot in August" is a filable fact with no number in it, and a model that can
      only record it as a quantity records it as nothing.

      ⭐⭐⭐ THE REMAINDER DECOMPOSES, AND THE TWO PARTS ARE DIFFERENT KINDS OF FACT.
      Writing `q` for the quantum, `k = nameplate / q` for the number of units held,
      and `m = k - floor(demand / q)`:

        r  =  m·q  -  (demand mod q)

        m·q             WHOLE QUANTA, AND A DECISION. Hold one more unit and it
                        moves. Who is allowed to make that decision is exactly
                        `Nameplate/amountOrigin`.
        (demand mod q)  THE RESIDUE, AND NOT A DECISION. No choice of k removes it.
                        The closest any procurement reaches is
                        `min(demand mod q, q - (demand mod q))`, the distance from
                        demand to the nearest whole multiple.

      ⛔⛔ SO THE CONSERVATION CLAIM IS ABOUT THE SECOND TERM ONLY. "Management
      chooses which absorber holds it and who bears it, never whether it exists" is
      TRUE OF THE RESIDUE AND FALSE OF THE WHOLE REMAINDER, because the whole
      remainder contains a decision. Asserting it of the total overclaims, and it is
      the kind of overclaim a reader holding the arithmetic finds in one line.

      ⭐⭐ THE MODULUS IS PRESENT ON BOTH BRANCHES, WHICH IS WHY THE NAME DESCRIBES
      THE WHOLE MODEL AND NOT HALF OF IT. Because the nameplate is a whole multiple,
      `r ≡ -demand (mod q)` ALWAYS. Rounding up leaves `(-demand) mod q` in [0, q);
      rounding down leaves `-(demand mod q)` in (-q, 0]. The two are additive
      inverses in the quotient and they sum to q, so clearance and interference are
      ONE DIVISION READ FROM OPPOSITE SIDES. ⭐ The sign is then the direction of the
      input still outstanding: a positive remainder is capacity already put in and
      not consumed, a negative one is capacity still owed.

      ⛔ THE NAMEPLATE MUST BE A WHOLE MULTIPLE OF THE QUANTUM for a lumpy supply.
      It is what makes the congruence above hold, and a nameplate off the lattice has
      no integer part to decompose. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no
      `xs:assert` and cannot compare across elements. An implementer still owes this
      check; the schema cannot take it from them.

      ⛔ `quantity` IS THE TOTAL, AND IT IS `derived` WHEREVER DEMAND, NAMEPLATE AND
      THE QUANTUM ARE ALL STATED. It is computable there, and a carried copy can
      disagree with its own inputs, which is the rule already applied to a Claim's
      expected value. ⚠️ It stays a StatedClaim rather than being deleted because a
      witness may know the total while filing no demand range at all — "we were about
      two people short" — which is the same case that keeps `sign`.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      ⛔ `quantity` IS EVALUATED ACROSS THE WHOLE DEMAND RANGE, WHILE `sign` IS
      EVALUATED AT `mostLikely`. Two conventions, and the difference is stated in
      full at Fit, which is where a reader meets the sign. ⚠️ The consequence worth
      carrying here: where `nameplate - demand` crosses zero the magnitude's LOW
      BOUND IS ZERO, because the difference passes through nothing on its way across.
      That zero is a fact about the top of the demand range and never a clamp.

      ⭐ CLEARANCE ACROSS THE WHOLE DEMAND RANGE RULES OUT TWO OF THE FIVE HOLDERS.
      With the nameplate above demand there is no unmet demand, so `customer` and
      `unrealised` cannot hold it: nobody waited and nothing went unserved. This
      constrains the branch the `capacitySlack` rule in Fit does not reach.

      ⛔⛔ `people` IS NOT RULED OUT, AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS RULE EXCLUDED IT
      WRONGLY. That draft assumed the only way a person bears a remainder is by
      absorbing excess work. UNDER-USE IS BORNE TOO: a team held persistently above
      its demand stagnates, stops learning and leaves, and no instrument records
      that either. It is the same structure as the interference case — a real cost,
      borne by people, with no transaction anywhere — and it is arguably the better
      demonstration of this model's own axis, because overwork is a story the world
      already tells and under-use is not.

      ⚠️ It is also the same absorber. Idle capacity IS the capacity buffer, so
      `booked` and `people` can hold one clearance remainder between them with
      `capacity` absorbing it either way. That is what two independent axes look
      like: the buffer does not determine the bearer.

      ⭐ THE TEST IS `sign` ITSELF NOW, AND IT USED TO NEED SPELLING OUT. While `Fit`
      had two members the sign was read at `mostLikely` while the magnitude was read
      across the range, so a layer whose demand range spanned its own nameplate filed
      `clearance` at the mode with part of its range still above the nameplate — and a
      rule keyed to `sign` would have rejected those documents wrongly. This one
      therefore said "across the whole demand range" in prose. That prose WAS ISO 286's
      criterion, and with `transition` filed the value carries it: `clearance` now means
      the whole range clears, and the spanning case has its own member.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      ⭐ `holder` IS UNBOUNDED because one remainder routinely lands on several
      parties at once. See Holder/share. ⚠️ A mixed list is meaningful rather than
      malformed: a stated `people` beside an `absent` entry reads "people bore some
      of it, and the rest was borne by somebody this sender cannot name", which is a
      better document than either half alone.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="sign"     type="pm:StatedFit"/>
      <xs:element name="absorber" type="pm:StatedBorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="holder"   type="pm:StatedHolder" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="quantity" type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       FIT: the sign of a remainder. ISO 286 vocabulary, borrowed for a reason.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="Fit">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      From ISO 286 (limits and fits), and the borrowing is not decoration.

      ⛔ THE FIT COMPARES THE DEMAND TO THE NAMEPLATE, NOT THE DRAW TO THE
      NAMEPLATE, WHICH IS THE EASY MISREADING AND GIVES THE WRONG ANSWER EVERY TIME
      A SUPPLY IS SATURATED.

        clearance     the nameplate exceeds the demand: slack, idle, undrawn.
        transition    the two ranges OVERLAP: some of the range each way.
        interference  the demand exceeds the nameplate.

      ⛔⛔ AND THE COMPARISON IS BETWEEN TWO RANGES, NOT TWO POINTS. This is ISO's
      own criterion, where a fit class is decided by how the hole's tolerance zone
      and the shaft's lie against each other, and it decides all three:

        clearance     nameplate.low  ≥ demand.high    the WHOLE range clears
        transition    the ranges overlap               partly each way
        interference  nameplate.high ≤ demand.low      the WHOLE range interferes

      ⭐ Total and exhaustive: three cases, mutually exclusive, covering every pair
      of ranges. `mostLikely` decides nothing here, which is the point of the next
      paragraph.

      ⭐ WHAT AN INTERFERENCE FIT DOES IS THE WHOLE REASON FOR THE WORD: it is not
      a failure, the assembly still works, AND IT WORKS BY DEFORMING THE MATERIAL.
      The deformation is permanent, it is the mechanism by which the fit functions,
      and inspecting the output will not reveal it.

      ⭐⭐ AND THAT MAPS EXACTLY, WHICH IS WHY THE WORD IS BORROWED RATHER THAN
      COINED. Demand is the shaft, the nameplate is the hole, and THE DRAW IS
      WHETHER IT ASSEMBLED:

        interference, draw > nameplate    it assembled by deforming. Somebody
                                          absorbed the excess: `people`, or
                                          `booked` where a penalty rate applied.
        interference, draw = nameplate    it did not assemble. The excess demand went
                                          UNSERVED: `customer` or `unrealised`.

      ⭐⭐ "UNSERVED" AND NOT "REFUSED" OR "TURNED AWAY", AND THE WORD IS LOAD-BEARING
      BECAUSE BOTH OF THE OTHER TWO NAME AN ACTOR. A reserved card that errors a request
      did refuse it. A caller who waits past their patience and goes elsewhere was
      refused by nobody — they left, and Layer/timeSlack already says so in as many
      words: THE HOLDER DOES NOT GET TO REFUSE, the demand DECAYED. Both land in the
      same two holders, so the word covering them must not decide which happened.
      ⭐ AND THE AXIS BETWEEN THOSE TWO HOLDERS IS WHETHER ANYBODY EXPERIENCED IT:
      `customer` is demand that was there and got degraded — the caller who waited and
      left — while `unrealised` never became anybody's experience at all.

      ⭐ SO THE DRAW IS NOT A THIRD REMAINDER; IT IS THE EVIDENCE FOR WHO HELD THE
      ONE REMAINDER. The magnitude is |nameplate - demand| either way, which is the
      conservation claim: management selects the holder, never the existence.

      ⭐⭐ ONE CONVENTION, AND IT TOOK THE THIRD MEMBER TO GET THERE. The sign and
      the magnitude are BOTH evaluated across the whole range. That reads as
      unremarkable and was not always true: while this was a two-member enumeration
      it took ONE value and therefore had to be read at ONE point, `mostLikely`,
      while the magnitude was computed over the range. Two conventions in one type,
      and the case that exposed them is the ordinary condition of a business AT
      capacity — a demand range that spans its own nameplate:

        demand [11.0, 13.2, 16.4], nameplate 16
        n - d      = [-0.4, 2.8, 5.0]      the ranges OVERLAP
        sign       = transition            0.4 short at the top, 5.0 spare at the
                                           bottom, and both are true of one layer
        |n - d|    = [0.0, 2.8, 5.0]       across the range

      ⭐ THE `0.0` IS ARITHMETIC, NOT A CLAMP. Where the difference crosses zero it
      genuinely passes through nothing, so the magnitude's minimum IS zero. What used
      to need defending is now said by the sign: a reader who sees `transition` knows
      to expect it.

      ⛔⛔ AND UNDER `transition` THE MAGNITUDE IS LOSSY, WHICH IS THE ONE THING A
      READER OF THIS ELEMENT MUST TAKE AWAY. `|n - d|` is sign-blind. That is
      harmless under the other two members, where the sign says which side the whole
      thing is on, but under `transition` there are TWO quantities and the absolute
      value keeps only the LARGER, with the smaller invisible inside it:

        clearance side    16 - 11.0 = 5.0     sets the high bound
        interference side 16.4 - 16 = 0.4     lies INSIDE [0.0, 5.0], indistinguishable
                                              from 0.4 of clearance

      Push the demand to [11.0, 13.2, 30] and it flips: the high bound becomes
      interference and the clearance side is the hidden one. Whichever side is larger
      sets the bound and erases the other.

      ⭐⭐ SO THE INTERFERENCE EXPOSURE IS DERIVED FROM `demand` AND `nameplate`
      DIRECTLY, AND NEVER FROM `quantity` OR THE SHARES:

        exposure = max(0, demand.high - nameplate.low)      = 0.4 here

      See Nameplate/capacitySlack, where that figure earns its keep.

      ⚠️ THE TWO SIDES ARE ANTI-CORRELATED AND MUST NOT BE ADDED. Clearance falls as
      demand rises and interference rises, so a component-wise sum pairs the slack
      week's spare with the busy week's unserved demand and reports a total that occurs in no
      week. It is the same class of error the composition rules already name, where
      one conversion factor multiplies both the nameplate and the demand and
      differencing them counts its spread twice — except here the shared driver is
      the demand itself and the pairing is exactly backwards. ⭐ THE CURE IS TO
      EVALUATE AT ONE CORNER: at a single demand there is one clearance value and one
      interference value, and nothing is summed across states. This is why `quantity`
      is one Claim and not two.

      ⭐⭐ WHY `transition` IS ADMITTED WHERE `indeterminate` IS REFUSED, since the
      obvious objection is that both are computable by the receiver. An
      `indeterminate` member states what the FILER DOES NOT KNOW, which is a
      conclusion the receiver reaches better on their own — the same objection that
      keeps a Claim's expected value out of the model. `transition` states what THE
      WORLD DOES: sometimes short, sometimes spare. ⚠️ And the derivability objection
      could never have carried the argument on its own, because `clearance` is
      equally computable, which is exactly why StatedFit lets `sign` say `derived`.
      The reason `sign` is carried at all is stated there: the DIRECTION is often
      known when the MAGNITUDE is not. "We ran hot in August" is a filable fact with
      no number in it, and so is "some weeks we are short and some weeks we are
      fine".

      ⛔ `sign` AGREES WITH THE RANGE COMPARISON ABOVE, not with `nameplate - demand`
      at any single point.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      ⚠️ Which supplies may sit on the interference side at all is a property of the
      supply. See Nameplate/capacitySlack. A supply whose `capacitySlack` is
      `absent reason="none"` — somebody looked, and there is no room above the rating
      — CANNOT have absorbed an interference fit, so EVERY ONE of its holders must be
      `customer` or `unrealised`, and a document saying otherwise is internally
      inconsistent. ⭐ A STATED SLACK BOUNDS RATHER THAN FORBIDS: holders may then sit
      on the interference side up to it and no further. ⚠️ The rule is over the whole list
      since `holder` became unbounded: one conforming entry does not excuse a second
      that claims the excess was absorbed by a supply that cannot absorb. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      ⭐⭐ UNDER `transition` THAT RULE BECOMES A PRESENCE RULE RATHER THAN A
      UNIVERSAL ONE, AND THE WEAKENING IS CORRECT RATHER THAN A CONCESSION. Part of
      the range is genuinely clearance, so a `booked` share is legitimate there: a
      reserved block paid for and not fully drawn is exactly that. What cannot happen
      is for the interference side to have been absorbed by a supply with no room
      above its rating. So:

        capacitySlack `absent reason="none"`, `sign` = transition, exposure > 0
          ==> AT LEAST ONE holder of kind `customer` or `unrealised`, the two that
              bear demand nobody served

      Some of the range is above the nameplate, none of it can have been absorbed, so
      that demand went unserved and the unserved share must appear on the list. ⚠️ WHAT CANNOT BE
      CHECKED IS ITS SIZE, and the reason is the lossiness above: the interference
      portion of a share is not a filed field, and the magnitude the shares sum to has
      swallowed it. The exposure bounds it — see Nameplate/capacitySlack — but that
      figure comes from `demand` and `nameplate`, not from anything on this remainder.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR, for the same reason as its interference twin.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="clearance"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="transition"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="interference"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       HOLDER: who BEARS a remainder. Incidence, not absorption.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="HolderKind">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      One variant is transacted inside the entity and four are not; that asymmetry
      is the central claim. A standard is entitled to disagree with any of the four,
      and each has its own reason:

        booked        not declined: a transacted position inside the entity
        counterparty  the burden sits in another entity's books under an AGREEMENT,
                      and THE CONSIDERATION NEED NOT HAVE BEEN CASH
        customer      no transaction occurred: degradation borne by a customer
                      creates no obligation of ours
        people        absorbed work is not a transaction and obliges the entity
                      to nobody
        unrealised    demand never served is not an event

      ⭐ A TYPE IS A CLAIM ABOUT WHAT CAN BE SAID, NOT A CLAIM THAT ANYTHING HAS
      BEEN MEASURED. Five variants assert that a bearer is one of five things. They
      assert nothing about how much lands on each.

      ⭐⭐ WHY `people` IS THE ONE WITHOUT AN INSTRUMENT, STATED AS A MECHANISM
      RATHER THAN AN ASSERTION: an excess becomes visible when there is a
      COUNTERPARTY WHO CAN INVOICE IT. A customer running over an allowance can be
      billed. A bank charges a penalty rate on an overdraft. A person cannot invoice
      you for the same thing, so the identical physical excess leaves no
      transaction. ⚠️ That also predicts where the argument STOPS: hire a
      contractor and the same absorbed work becomes `counterparty`: priced,
      invoiced, visible. The invisibility is a property of the RELATIONSHIP, not of
      the work.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="booked"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="counterparty"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="customer"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="people"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="unrealised"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <xs:complexType name="Holder">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔ NORMALISED ON EXTRACTION. The implementation this was drawn from carried
      Holder as a bare enum, because its consumer never read the payload. But
      `counterparty` asserts a fact about ANOTHER entity's books, which is a cached
      value nothing refreshes, so the party and the date it was true of belong
      WITH it. A flattening that was safe in one consumer must not be published as
      the model's shape.

      ⛔ party/asOf are permitted only where kind = counterparty. Expressed here as
      optional; an implementation should refuse the combination. ⚠️ IN A CONSOLIDATION
      THAT RULE BITES SOMEWHERE UNOBVIOUS, and it is worth stating because the mistake is
      natural: a `booked` share in a group filing is booked in SOME MEMBER'S books, and
      naming which one looks like exactly what `party` is for. It is not. On a
      `counterparty` holder `party` names WHOSE OTHER BOOKS the burden sits in; on a
      `booked` holder it would name WHICH OF OUR OWN UNITS records it. Two relations, one
      field. ⭐ The second is already carried, and carried better, by the composition that
      names which filed layer the share came from.

      ⭐⭐ AND THE RULE HAS A SECOND HALF THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIRST: A
      `counterparty` HOLDER MUST NAME ITS `party`. A burden asserted to sit in another
      entity's books, with no entity named, is not a counterparty claim — it is a guess
      wearing the one holder kind that promises an instrument. ⚠️ And it SHOULD carry
      `asOf`, by this type's own argument above: the value is a cached fact about somebody
      else's books and nothing here refreshes it, so a party with no date is a stale
      reading presented as a current one.

      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 cannot make one element's presence depend
      on a sibling's value. An implementer still owes both halves.

      ⚠️ THE REPAIR THAT WOULD REACH IT WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED, recorded so it is not
      re-proposed. Splitting `StatedHolder` into a three-way choice — `holder` carrying the
      other four kinds with no `party` element at all, `counterparty` carrying a REQUIRED
      party and asOf, and `absent` — makes both halves structurally unbreakable and checked
      by any stock validator. ⛔ It also leaves four variants in an enum and the fifth in
      the structure, which destroys HolderKind's central claim that these are FIVE VARIANTS
      OF ONE THING whose asymmetry is the finding. A rule enforced at the cost of the set it
      is a rule about is a bad trade.

      ⭐⭐⭐ `share` IS WHAT THIS HOLDER BEARS, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE ONE REMAINDER
      ROUTINELY LANDS ON MORE THAN ONE PARTY. A team that absorbs some of an excess
      and turns the rest away has split it between `people` and `unrealised`, and a
      single holder forced the sender to pick the larger one and discard the other.
      That discarded half is frequently the interesting one.

      ⭐⭐ A QUANTITY, NEVER A FRACTION, AND THE REASON IS THE MODEL'S OWN SUBJECT.
      A set of fractions must sum to one, so a single UNMEASURED member invalidates
      the whole set and the sender is pushed back to inventing a number. Quantities
      degrade honestly: `people` may bear an unmeasured share beside a `customer`
      share that somebody counted, and both survive transmission. A fraction would
      also drop the unit, and this model has no dimensionless number type anywhere.

      ⛔ THE SHARES SUM TO THE REMAINDER MAGNITUDE, `|nameplate - demand|`, WHEREVER
      EVERY SHARE IS STATED. One unmeasured share suspends the check rather than
      breaking it, which is the correct behaviour: the sum is unknown, not wrong.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      ⚠️ A KIND APPEARS AT MOST ONCE PER REMAINDER. Two `people` entries with two
      shares is not a finer split, it is one share written twice.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. An identity constraint cannot be scoped to a
      single remainder in XSD 1.0, because constraints attach to element declarations
      and this element is local to a reused type.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="kind"  type="pm:HolderKind"/>
      <xs:element name="share" type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="party" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="asOf"  type="xs:date"   minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       ABSENCE: a blank that says WHICH KIND OF BLANK IT IS.

       ⛔ THE POINT OF THE WHOLE SCHEMA, AND WHAT SEPARATES IT FROM AN ERP FORM.
       An ERP takes a number or nothing, so a receiver cannot tell "we do not
       measure this" from "this is genuinely zero" from "you are asking the wrong
       entity". Those are three different truths and only one of them is a gap.

       ⭐ AND IT MAKES REQUIRING AN ELEMENT CHEAP. A required element is expensive
       only when the sender must INVENT a value to satisfy it. Once a typed absence
       is a legitimate answer, a sender can always answer honestly, so the element
       stays required, and SILENCE BECOMES IMPOSSIBLE. That is what lets an absence
       survive transmission through a chain of organisations instead of decaying
       into a missing tag somebody downstream fills in with a guess.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="AbsenceReason">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      none          The answer IS nothing, and that is a claim rather than a gap.
                    Somebody looked and the quantity is zero, or the thing does not
                    happen. A receiver may rely on this.

      unmeasured    No instrument exists for this, here. The honest gap, and the
                    only one of the four that filling would resolve. It asserts
                    nothing about whether the quantity is large or small.

      notApplicable The subject is outside this question's population, so asking is
                    MALFORMED rather than unanswered. A receiver that treats this
                    as a gap will report a deficiency that does not exist.

      derived       This is an OUTPUT, computed from other elements. Sending a value
                    here would short-circuit the derivation and could disagree with
                    its own inputs. The receiver computes it.

      ⚠️ `unmeasured` and `none` are the pair most often merged, and merging them is
      expensive: one says nobody looked, the other says somebody looked and found
      nothing. A model that renders them identically cannot tell an absence of
      evidence from evidence of absence.

      ⭐⭐ AND THE PAIR IS LOAD-BEARING IN THIS MODEL SPECIFICALLY. The central
      argument is that one buffer is consumed without a transaction and therefore
      has no instrument. `unmeasured` on a labour draw IS that argument, filed. If a
      sender can only leave the field blank, the claim and an oversight look
      identical, and the model's own subject becomes unrecordable.

      ⭐⭐⭐ FOUR, AND DELIBERATELY NOT SIX. ASKED AND ANSWERED, SO THAT THE NEXT
      ADOPTER DOES NOT HAVE TO ASK IT AGAIN. An adopting corpus filed seven absence
      values, of which two had no home here. Both were examined and both were
      declined, for DIFFERENT reasons:

        `declined`    somebody was asked and refused. The REASON is still
                      `unmeasured` (no instrument reached the value), and what is
                      missing is WHY nobody measured, which is provenance rather
                      than a fifth reason. It is a fact about a RELATIONSHIP.

        `unverified`  ⛔ NOT AN ABSENCE AT ALL. A value filed but unverified HAS a
                      value. Admitting it here would let a present figure claim to
                      be absent, which is strictly worse than the gap it closes.
                      It is a fact about the STANDING of an assertion, and standing
                      belongs on the assertion. See `Assertion/standing`.

      ⚠️ Two independent models arrived at exactly these four classes with
      near-identical glosses, which is evidence and is why the bar for a fifth is
      high. ⭐ Frequency is NOT part of that bar: an adopting corpus exercised both
      orphans and used `none` in zero of 47 registers, and neither figure changes
      the reasoning above.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="none"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="unmeasured"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="notApplicable"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="derived"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <xs:complexType name="Provenance">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      WHO stands behind a value, and WHAT STANDING that assertion has.

      ⛔ THIS REPLACED A BARE `asserter` STRING, AND THE FLATTENING IT FIXES IS
      REAL. Two independent adopting corpora file provenance as THREE columns:
      the entity, the person who ENTERED the figure, and the person who APPROVED
      it. All three landing in one free-text field is lossy in the way that costs
      most: a signer whose name is spelled two ways in two places becomes
      undetectable rather than joinable, because there is no identity to join on.

      ⭐ THE SCHEMA ALREADY FIXED THIS EXACT FLATTENING ONE TYPE OVER. `Holder` is
      { kind, party?, asOf? } rather than a string, on the reasoning that WHO is a
      separate fact from WHAT KIND. This is the same shape, and it did not get the
      same treatment until an adopter showed the cost.

      ⭐⭐ `standing` IS WHERE `unverified` BELONGS, and it is why that value is not
      an AbsenceReason. A figure that is filed but unverified HAS a value; what is
      missing is the standing of the assertion, not the quantity. Entered,
      reviewed, approved, verified against a source are points on that axis.

      ⚠️ `standing` IS A BorrowedTerm RATHER THAN AN ENUMERATION, deliberately and
      on the expectation that it varies by framework. This model does not own the
      set, and restating one it does not own is the mistake `BorrowedTerm` exists
      to prevent.

      ⛔⛔ AND IT IS REQUIRED-AND-WRAPPED RATHER THAN OPTIONAL, WHICH IS A CHANGE
      `asrt:Composition` FORCED. That type says in its own words that a composition
      filed without `provenance/standing` IS A FABRICATION — and both were optional
      for three revisions, so the schema named the condition and then permitted
      documents that fail it. ⭐ The deeper reason is that WHAT A LAYER MEANS DEPENDS
      ON WHO FILED IT: a parent's composition asserts a group's system, an auditor's
      asserts what an auditor holds, and the same layer in the two is not the same
      claim. Standing is the only field that says which you are reading, so its
      silence is the most expensive silence in the model.

      ⭐ `absent reason="unmeasured"` is the honest answer for a sender whose framework
      publishes no standing vocabulary, and `notApplicable` for an assertion nobody
      is standing behind in any formal sense. Both say more than a blank.

      `note` is prose BESIDE the structured fields, never instead of them, the
      same rule `Absence` states about its own note.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="party"      type="xs:token"       minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="enteredBy"  type="xs:token"       minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="approvedBy" type="xs:token"       minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="standing"   type="pm:StatedBorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="note"       type="xs:string"      minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Absence">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      `asserter` / `asOf` matter here for the same reason they matter on a Claim:
      in a chain, WHO says a thing is unmeasured is itself information. One party
      not measuring something is not evidence that the party upstream does not.

      ⭐⭐⭐ A REASON NO QUERY CAN REACH IS NOT A TYPED ABSENCE. `reason` is the
      element that carries the meaning; `note` is prose beside it and never
      instead of it. An adopting corpus was measured against exactly this
      definition (does a blank column have a SIBLING a query can join on) and
      scored 0 of 540 blanks, because every explanation lived in free text.

      ⛔ ITS BEST SPECIMEN IS THE CLEAREST CASE: one register files a legal form
      empty on purpose, with an excellent paragraph beside it saying why. NOTHING
      CAN JOIN ON THAT PARAGRAPH. An adopter who puts the reason in a note has met
      this schema's letter and lost its entire benefit, which is why the rule is
      stated here rather than assumed.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="reason"   type="pm:AbsenceReason"/>
      <xs:element name="note"     type="xs:string" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="provenance" type="pm:Provenance" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="asOf"     type="xs:date"   minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       STATED: a value, or a typed reason there is none. Never silence.

       ⚠️ XSD 1.0 has no generics, so there is one wrapper per stated type.
       The verbosity is the cost of the guarantee.

       ⭐ A WRAPPER EXISTS ONLY WHERE A SENDER MAY LEGITIMATELY NOT HAVE THE VALUE,
       and DECLINING to add one is the assertion that this value is always knowable
       by whoever is sending. Eleven exist. Not wrapped, deliberately: Nameplate and
       Jagged (their own leaf quantities are stated, so wrapping the container as
       well would give one fact two homes).
       ⚠️ Fit WAS on that list — "the direction is known whenever the remainder is" —
       and StatedFit replaced it. Two of the three exceptions this block once named
       have now fallen, both because the reasoning was about a SENDER WHO HAS THE
       FACT rather than about the range of senders. That is worth a reader's
       suspicion of the one remaining.

       ⛔⛔⛔ AND A REPEATING ELEMENT NEEDS A WRAPPER MORE THAN A SINGLE ONE DOES, WHICH
       THIS BLOCK MISSED FOR TWO REVISIONS. `minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"` looks
       like it has already solved absence, because a sender who has nothing simply
       writes nothing — and that is precisely the failure. AN EMPTY LIST IS A BLANK
       FIELD WEARING A PLURAL. It cannot distinguish

         somebody looked and there are none      `none`
         nobody looked                           `unmeasured`
         this document is the wrong place to ask `notApplicable`

       and for the two lists in this model that carry FALSIFIERS — `Stack/coupling`
       and `asrt:Fusion/elimination` — the first two readings are opposite verdicts on
       the model itself. `StatedCouplings` and `asrt:StatedEliminations` are the repair:
       one branch is the list with at least one member, the other is an Absence, and the
       empty document is no longer writable.

       ⛔ AND FOR BorrowedTerm THE RULE IS NOT "OPTIONAL VERSUS REQUIRED". THIS BLOCK
       SAID THAT, AND THE SCHEMA CONTRADICTS IT: `Remainder/absorber` and
       `Citation/instrument` are both REQUIRED and both unwrapped. The rule that
       actually predicts every site:

         wrapped     where the `none` / `unmeasured` distinction is load-bearing AT
                     THAT POSITION and no enclosing typed absence already carries it.
                     `Regime/framework` and `Regime/chart` are the two. A Regime
                     cannot be declined with a reason, only omitted, and it is
                     unbounded, so wrapping the whole type would produce A LIST OF
                     MAYBE-REGIMES, which says less than the list it replaced.

         unwrapped   where an enclosing Stated* already says it: `absorber` under
                     StatedRemainder, `borrowed` under StatedBasis, `holds` beside
                     `cannotAsk`, OR where the term is an ADDITION to a fact rather
                     than the FRAME the fact is read under, so its silence says only
                     "not cited": `Citation/instrument`, `Provenance/standing`.

       ⚠️ Divisibility WAS on this list, on the reasoning that a sender describing a
       supply knows whether they buy it in whole units. That was wrong about a
       subject which is not a supply at all, and StatedDivisibility replaced it.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="StatedClaim">
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="claim"  type="pm:Claim"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedHolder">
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="holder" type="pm:Holder"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedBasis">
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="basis"  type="pm:MeasurementBasis"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedRemainder">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐ THE WRAPPER THAT LETS THE MODEL BE CONTRADICTED, AND IT IS WHY EVERY LAYER
      IS REQUIRED TO CARRY ONE.

      The model claims every layer has a remainder. Requiring the element forces a
      sender who disagrees to say so EXPLICITLY. `absent reason="none"` reads
      "somebody looked at this layer and there is no remainder", which is a filed
      counter-example rather than an empty field.

      ⛔ Without the wrapper, a required Remainder would force the sender to invent
      an absorber and a holder for a difference they say does not exist, and an
      invented value is exactly what typed absence exists to prevent.

      `unmeasured` is the honest and commonest answer on a labour layer.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="remainder" type="pm:Remainder"/>
      <xs:element name="absent"    type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedBorrowedTerm">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔ FOR THE TWO PLACES A BORROWED TERM IS THE FRAME A DOCUMENT IS READ UNDER AND
      A SENDER MAY LEGITIMATELY NOT HAVE IT YET: `Regime/framework` and
      `Regime/chart`. ⚠️ NOT because they are required: `Remainder/absorber` and
      `Citation/instrument` are required and unwrapped too. It is because nothing
      above them can decline, and because their silence would DISABLE A CHECK rather
      than merely leave something uncited. See the STATED block for the rule.

      ⭐⭐ WITHOUT THIS, `none` AND `unmeasured` SHARE ONE ENCODING AT THE ROOT OF
      INTERPRETATION. A sender whose tier is unassigned, whose legal form is filed
      empty on purpose, or whose coding authority has not answered had two moves:
      invent a framework value, which files a fact nobody established, or omit the
      regime entirely, which is byte-identical to reporting under no framework.

      ⭐ With the wrapper the two separate cleanly, and `regime` being unbounded is
      what completes it:

        regime omitted                          nobody said
        framework absent reason="unmeasured"    reports under something, unnamed
        framework absent reason="none"          somebody looked; reports under none

      ⭐ `Regime/chart` takes the same four-way shape for the same reason, and its own
      annotation carries the case that forced it: a chart in daily use with no
      publishing authority to borrow from.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="term"   type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedDivisibility">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔ SOME SUBJECTS HAVE NO DIVISIBILITY AT ALL, BECAUSE THEY ARE NOT SUPPLIES.
      A margin ratio is neither bought in whole units nor bought continuously:
      there is no per-unit difference between the two, so there is no premium to
      pay for leaving the type. The axis does not apply.

      ⛔⛔ WITHOUT THE WRAPPER THE ONLY WAY TO FILE THAT IS TO ASSERT `continuous`
      AND DENY IT ONE LEVEL DOWN, in the premium's own typed absence. The document
      then asserts something it does not believe, and the denial sits where no
      query will meet it, while `notApplicable`'s annotation names the cost:
      "a receiver that treats this as a gap will report a deficiency that does not
      exist."

      ⚠️ NOT a third branch of the choice. `notApplicable` is not a kind of
      divisibility; it is the absence of one, and absence already has a type.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="divisibility" type="pm:Divisibility"/>
      <xs:element name="absent"       type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedConstraintOrigin">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      For `Nameplate/amountOrigin`, and WRAPPED WHERE `LumpyQuantum/origin` IS NOT.
      The difference is not an oversight and it is worth stating, because the two
      elements carry the same enumeration.

      ⭐ A QUANTUM'S ORIGIN IS A FACT ABOUT THE SUPPLY. A sender who filed `lumpy`
      has already established the block size, and knowing the size means knowing
      whose terms set it. There is no honest state where the size is known and its
      author is not.

      ⭐⭐ AN AMOUNT'S ORIGIN IS A COUNTERFACTUAL: could we have held a different
      number of them? That is a question about a decision nobody may have tested,
      and a new sender frequently has not. Forcing a value there would make an
      untested assumption indistinguishable from an established one, which is the
      bargain typed absence exists to protect.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND `Claim/boundOrigin` IS THE SAME COUNTERFACTUAL ABOUT A DIFFERENT
      QUANTITY, WHICH IS WHY IT NOW TAKES THIS TYPE AND USED TO TAKE A BARE OPTIONAL
      ENUMERATION. `amountOrigin` asks who could hold a different number of units;
      `boundOrigin` asks who could move the edge of a range. One question, two
      quantities — and for two revisions one of them was required-and-wrapped while
      the other was optional-and-silent, three lines apart in the same sequence.

      ⛔ THE COST OF THE SILENT VERSION IS NAMED IN `Nameplate/capacitySlack`, which
      complains that the three buffer slacks "say how much room a buffer has and
      NOTHING about whether anybody can move it", observes that the slot already
      exists, and then reports it filed ONCE IN THE WHOLE CORPUS. An optional field
      that nobody fills is not a weak signal; it is an absent one, and it is absent
      for the ordinary reason optional fields are.

      ⭐ AND THE FOUR REASONS ALL LAND ON A BOUND, WHICH IS WHY REQUIRING IT COSTS A
      SENDER NOTHING HONEST:

        origin intrinsic/contractual/policy  somebody can move this, and here is who
        absent reason="unmeasured"           nobody has asked who sets this bound
        absent reason="none"                 ⛔ NOTHING SETS IT. The bound is where the
                                             measurements fell, not where a rule put
                                             them, and there is no lever to look for
        absent reason="notApplicable"        the quantity has no author to ask about:
                                             an observed count, a derived residual
        absent reason="derived"              it follows from a bound stated elsewhere

      ⭐⭐ `none` IS THE MEMBER THAT DID NOT EXIST BEFORE AND IT IS THE COMMON CASE.
      A demand interval read off twelve months of history has bounds nobody chose; an
      SLA has bounds somebody negotiated. Both were the same blank.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="origin" type="pm:ConstraintOrigin"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedNotation">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ A FILING SAYING WHICH FILING IT IS, WHICH NOTHING IN THIS MODEL COULD DO UNTIL NOW.
      Recorded as S-28 and found by writing a foreign key: a key needs something to point at,
      and there was nothing there.

      ⭐ THE VALUE IS THE WHOLE URN A PART CITES, not a naming system plus an identifier.
      `asrt:Part` addresses a layer as `{notation, id}` where the NOTATION is the other
      filing's URN and the ID is the layer name inside it. So the thing a filing must be able
      to say about itself is exactly that notation, spelled identically, or the two never meet.

      ⭐⭐ AND IT IS WHAT MAKES A LOCAL PART POSSIBLE, WHICH IS THE POINT. A part is LOCAL when
      the notation it cites is this document's own — nothing else changes, no second kind of
      part, no new element on `asrt:Part`. A composer can then build a layer out of layers
      they built, in one document, and separate them again afterwards.

      ⚠️ WRAPPED RATHER THAN REQUIRED-AND-BARE, because a filing genuinely may not have one:

        uri                         this document is that URN, and a part may cite it
        absent reason="none"        a working document, published under no identifier at all
        absent reason="unmeasured"  it will have one and nobody has assigned it yet
        absent reason="notApplicable"  ⛔ RARE AND SUSPICIOUS. A document nobody may reference
                                    cannot be composed into anything, which is a strong claim

      ⛔ A FILING WITH NO NOTATION CANNOT BE A PART OF ANYTHING, and that is the honest
      consequence rather than a gap: a composition asserting a relationship to a filing nobody
      can identify is asserting a relationship to nothing. `assets/sql/ingest.sql` populates
      `filing_identity` from FILENAMES today and says in the file that this is the reader
      guessing; a filed notation is what stops it guessing.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR ACROSS DOCUMENTS. XSD 1.0 cannot follow a reference into
      another file. Inside ONE composition it is reachable, and it is checked.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="uri"    type="xs:anyURI"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:simpleType name="ScopeExtent">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ HOW MUCH OF THE SYSTEM THIS STACK HOLDS. There is ONE system; a filing holds the
      layers of it that mattered to whoever filed. **A FILING IS NEVER THE SYSTEM**, and until
      this element existed `pm:Stack` read as though it enumerated one.

        complete   somebody looked and the system HAS no other layers. ⭐ A heavy claim, and
                   the rare one: it asserts a boundary rather than a selection
        scoped     somebody looked, the system HAS others, and these are the ones this
                   document needs. The ordinary state of every third-party filing
        unbounded  nobody looked outside these. They are the ones needed and what else exists
                   was never asked

      ⭐⭐ THE THIRD MEMBER IS THE ONE A TWO-VALUED ENCODING WOULD CRUSH, and it is the same
      distinction `Stack/couplings` turns on one element away: `scoped` says somebody
      established what lies outside and excluded it; `unbounded` says nobody looked. A model
      that renders them identically cannot tell a bounded selection from an unexamined one.

      ⛔⛔ AND THIS IS WHAT MAKES A SECOND DOCUMENT'S EXTRA LAYER ORDINARY RATHER THAN AN
      ACCUSATION. An auditor composing a client's filing and adding a layer is filing a second
      PROJECTION of one system, not alleging the client omitted something. Without a declared
      scope every added layer reads as a finding against the filer, and the model would be
      picking a fight on the composer's behalf every time somebody looked at a process from a
      second angle.

      ⚠️ `complete` IS FALSIFIABLE AND SHOULD BE TREATED AS SUCH. Any later filing that holds a
      layer of the same system contradicts it, which is the property that makes it worth
      having and the reason it is not the default.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="complete"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="scoped"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="unbounded"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <xs:complexType name="Scope">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      WHAT DECIDED WHICH LAYERS ARE HERE, and how much of the system that leaves out.

      ⭐ SHAPED AFTER `Narrowing`, and for the same reason: a typed member beside required
      prose. The `extent` is what a query groups by; `basis` is what a reader needs in order to
      disagree. Neither is sufficient alone — an extent with no basis is an assertion with no
      argument, and a basis with no extent cannot be counted across a corpus.

      `basis` is REQUIRED. ⛔ "These are the layers an audit of the revenue cycle needs" and
      "these are the ones the client happened to file" are different documents, and the
      difference is invisible in the layer list.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="extent" type="pm:ScopeExtent"/>
      <xs:element name="basis"  type="xs:string"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedScope">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⚠️ THE FOURTH STATE, AND IT IS NOT `unbounded`. `unbounded` says the filer considered what
      lies outside and did not look; `absent reason="unmeasured"` says the question of extent
      was never put at all. The first is a filing decision, the second is a gap — the same
      distinction `Absence` draws everywhere else, arriving one level up.

      ⛔ `none` HAS NO MEANING HERE. A stack has at least one layer, so "there is no scope" is
      not a state a document can be in. See tests/state_coverage.rs, which refuses it.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="scope"  type="pm:Scope"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedLumpyQuantum">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔ FOR `Divisibility/window`, AND ITS FOUR STATES ARE ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN IN THAT
      ELEMENT'S OWN ANNOTATION — WHICH COULD NOT FILE THREE OF THEM. It says a window is
      MALFORMED on a unit with no denominator ("`12 people` has no period, so '5 days' has
      nothing to be five days of"), and it says a supply that is always on "does not need
      saying so". Those are `notApplicable` and `none`, described in prose, encoded
      identically as an omitted element, and indistinguishable from nobody having asked.

        quantum                     the supply is live for this much of each period
        absent reason="none"        somebody looked; the supply is always on. Duty
                                    fraction 1, and a `derived` timeSlack stays legal
        absent reason="unmeasured"  the unit HAS a period and nobody has measured the
                                    live part of it. ⛔ The one state that disables
                                    the timeSlack rule below
        absent reason="notApplicable"  the unit has no denominator, so there is no cycle
                                    to be live in part of. A stock, not a rate

      ⭐⭐ AND THE DISTINCTION BUYS BACK A RULE THE ELEMENT ITSELF ASKS FOR. Its last
      paragraph says "where a `window` is filed, `Layer/timeSlack` must not be `absent
      reason='derived'`", because a clearance spread evenly across the denominator is
      exactly what a window denies. `unmeasured` is the case that rule could not reach:
      nobody knows whether the spare is spread evenly, so a DERIVED time slack is an
      assumption rather than a computation. With `none` filed it is a computation again.

      ⚠️ NOT `derived`. A duty cycle is a fact about the supply's calendar and nothing
      else in the model implies one; admitting the reason here would invite a receiver
      to compute a window out of a slack, which is the inference the element forbids
      running in the other direction.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="quantum" type="pm:LumpyQuantum"/>
      <xs:element name="absent"  type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedCouplings">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ THE WRAPPER THE MODEL'S OWN FALSIFIER NEEDED, AND THE SCHEMA HAD ALREADY
      DIAGNOSED ITS ABSENCE WITHOUT BEING ABLE TO FILE THE CURE. `Coupling` says, in its
      own words: "ZERO COUPLINGS IS THE MODEL'S ASSUMPTION, NOT A PROVEN PROPERTY. A
      document with no couplings is not evidence of independence; it is a document where
      nobody looked." `Absence` says, in its own words: "one says nobody looked, the
      other says somebody looked and found nothing." Two annotations, one gap, and an
      element that could only write the ambiguous thing.

        coupling+                   somebody looked and found these
        absent reason="none"        ⭐⭐ SOMEBODY LOOKED AND THE LAYERS MOVE
                                    INDEPENDENTLY. This is the model's central claim,
                                    ASSERTED, and it was unfileable
        absent reason="unmeasured"  nobody has tested it. The honest state of most
                                    first filings, and what an empty list used to mean
                                    whether the filer intended it or not
        absent reason="notApplicable"  a one-layer stack. There is no pair to couple

      ⭐⭐⭐ THE ASYMMETRY IS WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN THE OTHER WRAPPERS. Everywhere
      else typed absence protects a RECEIVER from reading a gap as a zero. Here it
      protects the MODEL FROM ITSELF: a corpus of documents that all file `unmeasured`
      is a corpus in which the independence assumption has never been tested, and that
      is a fact about the evidence rather than about any one filing. It is now countable.

      ⚠️ AND `none` IS A HEAVY CLAIM RATHER THAN THE EASY DEFAULT. It says somebody
      relieved a layer's constraint and watched the others. A filer who has not done that
      owes `unmeasured`, and a corpus where `none` is common without a word in
      `Absence/note` about how it was established has swapped one silence for a bolder one.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="coupling" type="pm:Coupling" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="absent"   type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedFit">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔ THE CASE THIS WRAPPER WAS ORIGINALLY BUILT FOR TURNED OUT TO BE A FIT, AND
      THE CORRECTION IS RECORDED RATHER THAN QUIETLY SWEPT. A demand of [3, 4, 5]
      people against a nameplate of 4 was read here as an ABSENCE of fit, because the
      two-member enumeration was evaluated at `mostLikely` and that point sat at
      exactly zero. It is a `transition` fit: overlapping ranges at the nominal size,
      which is the textbook case rather than a boundary curiosity. `absent
      reason="none"` was doing the missing member's job, and it is not doing it any
      more. ⚠️ No document in `assets/corpus/` ever filed an absent `sign`, so the case
      being corrected here was argued and never exercised.

      ⭐ WHAT THE WRAPPER IS ACTUALLY FOR, both halves surviving intact. It lets
      `sign` say `derived`: where demand and nameplate are both stated the fit is
      computable, which is the same condition under which `quantity` is derived. One
      argument, applied to both elements instead of one.

      ⚠️ And the original defence of carrying `sign` at all still stands, which is why
      this is a wrapper rather than a deletion: the DIRECTION is frequently known when
      the MAGNITUDE is not. "We ran hot in August" is a filable fact with no number in
      it, and so is "some weeks we are short and some weeks we are fine" — which is
      `transition`, and is probably the more common of the two.

      ⭐ `absent` still has work: `unmeasured` where nobody compared the two, and
      `notApplicable` on a layer with no nameplate to compare against. What it no
      longer means is "the ranges overlap".
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="fit"    type="pm:Fit"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       CLAIM: how EVERY quantity in this model is expressed.

       ⛔ THERE IS NO BARE NUMBER TYPE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. A quantity crossing
       an organisational boundary is something somebody ASSERTS. Stripping it to a
       scalar discards the two things a receiver needs in order to evaluate it
       truthfully: how precise the assertion is, and what would change it.
       ================================================================== -->
  <!-- ==================================================================
       NARROWING: what would tighten a range, and WHICH KIND OF ACT that is.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="NarrowingKind">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐⭐ WHAT KIND OF ACT WOULD TIGHTEN THIS RANGE, AND IT IS NOT DECORATION: the
      answer says WHAT THE WIDTH IS MADE OF.

        instrument    measure it better. The width is IGNORANCE — the range was
                      always this wide and a better instrument reveals it.
                      "support interrupts are time-recorded instead of estimated"

        intervention  change the process. The width is VARIATION — the number really
                      does move, and only changing the world makes it move less.
                      "the members bill on a fixed 30-day cycle instead of a
                      calendar month", against a month of [672, 720, 744] hours.
                      No instrument measures that away.

        experiment    perturb it deliberately and see. THE FILER DOES NOT KNOW WHICH
                      OF THE OTHER TWO IT IS, and says so by naming what would tell
                      them. "the reservation is varied deliberately across two more
                      quarters"

      ⛔ THIS IS THE DISTINCTION THE MODEL WAS MISSING AND IT WAS HIDING IN PROSE. A
      `Claim`'s range reads as epistemic throughout — "most likely", "the value an
      estimator can honestly state" — but a range that is genuine variation does not
      narrow when you measure harder. The corpus already contained both kinds, told
      apart only by reading the sentence, which is exactly the place no rule reaches.

      ⭐ AND `experiment` IS WHY THIS IS NOT A TWO-VALUED FLAG. "Ignorance or
      variation?" is a question a filer frequently cannot answer, and forcing the
      choice would collect a guess. Naming the experiment that would settle it is a
      third honest answer, and it is the same move `Absence` makes everywhere else.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="instrument"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="intervention"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="experiment"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <xs:complexType name="Narrowing">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      The condition, and what kind of act it is. `condition` is prose and stays prose:
      no vocabulary could cover "the quarter closes and actual cost lands" and the
      attempt would fork somebody's accounting calendar. `kind` is the part a rule can
      reach.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="condition" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="kind"      type="pm:NarrowingKind"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedNarrowing">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔ REQUIRED ON EVERY CLAIM, AND THAT REVERSES AN EARLIER DECISION IN THIS FILE.
      `narrowsWhen` was an optional bare string, and the reason given was cost: "a
      required element is expensive in a chain of parties". That reasoning was written
      when the field was prose only. It did not know the BLANK was carrying a fact.

      ⭐ THE THREE ABSENCES ARE THREE DIFFERENT CLAIMS and the old blank meant all of
      them at once:

        absent reason="none"           somebody looked and NOTHING would tighten this.
                                       ⭐⭐ WHICH IS THE VARIATION CLAIM, STATED. The
                                       width is not ignorance, so no instrument, no
                                       intervention and no experiment reduces it.
        absent reason="unmeasured"     nobody has said what would tighten it. The
                                       honest default, and what the old blank usually
                                       meant.
        absent reason="notApplicable"  there is no range. low = mostLikely = high, so
                                       there is nothing to tighten.

      ⛔ OPTIONAL-WITH-A-WRAPPER WAS REJECTED, on this schema's own precedent. See
      Regime/chart: admitting BOTH an omitted element AND `absent reason="none"` is
      "two encodings of one fact with no rule saying which to send", and it leaves a
      receiver unable to tell "nothing would narrow this" from "nobody said" — which is
      precisely the distinction this element now exists to carry.

      ⚠️ THE COST IS REAL AND IT IS PAID BY EVERY SENDER. 124 claims in `assets/corpus/`
      each gained one, of which 59 are `notApplicable` because they are point values.
      That is the trade: a mechanical burden on every filing, in exchange for a fact
      that was otherwise unstatable.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="narrowing" type="pm:Narrowing"/>
      <xs:element name="absent"    type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Claim">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      A THREE-POINT ESTIMATE with its provenance.

      `mostLikely` is the MODE, the value an estimator can honestly state, and
      NEVER the median: on a skewed range they are different numbers, and a field
      that collects both is a slot with no domain.

      ⛔ THE BOUNDS MUST SATISFY `low` ≤ `mostLikely` ≤ `high`. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.
      A claim filed as 99 / 50 / 1 validates today and is nonsense.

      ⭐ A POINT VALUE IS THE DEGENERATE CASE: low = mostLikely = high, meaning
      "known exactly". It is precision stated honestly rather than a misuse of the
      type, and it is why one type covers both cases and no separate scalar exists.

      ⭐⭐ AND IT IS WHY A CONTINUOUS DEMAND NEEDS NO SECOND TYPE. "Between a fifth
      and a half of a person, most likely a third" is a demand that genuinely has no
      single value, and this is the type that says so. A model whose demand is a
      scalar has already rounded away the thing it exists to measure.

      ⛔ THE EXPECTED VALUE IS DERIVED AND MUST NOT BE CARRIED.
        triangular  E = (low + mostLikely + high) / 3
        PERT        E = (low + 4*mostLikely + high) / 6,  sigma ~ (high - low) / 6
      A stored E short-circuits the derivation and can disagree with its own inputs.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      `unit` CARRIES CURRENCY TOO: "EUR", "months", "share of annual salary" are
      all units here, and that is not a shortcut: the corpus this was drawn from
      files USD beside months and shares in one column, and has done so throughout.
      ⚠️ A unit is NOT a conversion licence. Currencies are never converted, and
      two claims in different units do not combine.

      `narrowsWhen` is what would tighten the bounds or move the mode, and it is the
      difference between a claim and a number pair. ⛔ IT USED TO BE AN OPTIONAL BARE
      STRING and it is now a required `StatedNarrowing`, because the blank was carrying
      a fact nothing else could state: a range that NOTHING would tighten is variation
      rather than ignorance, and an omitted element could not say so. See
      StatedNarrowing for the reversal and what it costs.

      `boundOrigin` is WHO COULD MOVE THE EDGE, and it is a required
      `StatedConstraintOrigin` for `narrowsWhen`'s reason one field over: as an
      optional bare enumeration it was filed once in 124 claims, and its blank could
      not separate "nobody has asked" from "nothing sets this bound — the range is
      where the measurements fell". ⭐ The two elements are one pair. `narrowsWhen`
      says what would make the range SMALLER; `boundOrigin` says who owns the edge it
      would move. A range with neither is a number pair with error bars.

      `asserter` / `asOf`: WHO says this and WHEN it was true. In a chain of
      organisations these are not bookkeeping: one party's claim about another
      party's capacity is a different fact from that party's own claim about it.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="low"         type="xs:decimal"/>
      <xs:element name="mostLikely"  type="xs:decimal"/>
      <xs:element name="high"        type="xs:decimal"/>
      <xs:element name="unit"        type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="narrowsWhen" type="pm:StatedNarrowing"/>
      <xs:element name="boundOrigin" type="pm:StatedConstraintOrigin"/>
      <xs:element name="provenance"  type="pm:Provenance" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="asOf"        type="xs:date"   minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       MEASUREMENT BASIS: HOW an amount was valued.

       ⛔ THIS SCHEMA DEFINES EXACTLY ONE BASIS. Every other basis belongs to the
       regime that defines it, and a document says which regime it took one from.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="ContributedBasis">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      The only measurement basis this model contributes, and the reason the type
      exists at all.

        nameplate   a DECLARED quantity. A rate card, or an 8-GPU reserved block,
                    is declared rather than measured. No accounting standard has
                    such a basis, so without it a declaration must masquerade as a
                    measurement, and then asking for its measurement basis looks
                    like a gap instead of a category error.

      ⚠️ A nameplate that sits in the Nameplate POSITION needs no basis element at
      all; the position says it. This value is for the case that actually causes
      trouble: a figure filed as a MEASURED draw that was in fact read off a rate
      card. That is a jagged face wearing a declared number, and naming it is the
      only way a receiver can tell.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="nameplate"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <xs:complexType name="MeasurementBasis">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      A basis is EITHER one this model contributes OR one borrowed from a regime,
      and the two are different kinds of statement rather than one shape with an
      optional discriminator, so this is a choice, not an optional attribute.

      ⛔ THE FIRST DRAFT GOT THIS WRONG AND THE ERROR IS WORTH RECORDING. It had a
      single element with an optional `taxonomy` and a `value` typed `xs:token`,
      and documented in prose that omitting the taxonomy meant the value had to be
      one this schema defines. Nothing enforced it: `xs:token` accepts anything, so
      ContributedBasis was defined and never referenced, a constraint that lived
      only in an annotation, which is a rule with no gate behind it.

      `contributed`   defined HERE, and this schema is answerable for it.
      `borrowed`      defined by the named taxonomy. This schema asserts NOTHING
                      about the value and does not restate the regime's list.
                      Restating it would fork somebody else's normative set.

      Examples of `taxonomy`: an IFRS or US-GAAP taxonomy URI, a SAF-T referencial,
      an IES AnexoASNC referencial.

      ⚠️ Why the taxonomy is REQUIRED on a borrowed basis: two published four-value
      declarations that do not line up. SAF-T's referencial (S · M · N · O) and
      IES's AnexoASNC (NIC · NCRF · NCRF-PE · NC-ME): NCRF and NCRF-PE both
      collapse into S, and O has no IES counterpart. A borrowed value without its
      taxonomy is genuinely AMBIGUOUS, not merely unattributed.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="contributed" type="pm:ContributedBasis"/>
      <xs:element name="borrowed"    type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="BorrowedTerm">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      A term defined elsewhere, carrying the taxonomy that defines it. Both parts
      are required: the value is meaningless without the authority it came from.

      ⭐ Used for every value this model declines to own: a measurement basis from
      an accounting framework, a buffer from the operations-science literature.
      Restating another body's normative list inside this namespace would fork it,
      and a fork drifts with nothing here able to notice.

      ⛔⛔ THE LIMITATION, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED: A SCHEMA CANNOT VALIDATE A
      TERM WHOSE AUTHORITY IS PROSE. Most accounting vocabulary is exactly that.
      `historicalCost`, `fairValue` and `netRealisable` are defined in STANDARDS
      TEXT (ASC 820, IFRS 13) and not as enumerated values in any published
      schema. An XBRL taxonomy publishes CONCEPTS, not an enum of measurement
      bases. So there is frequently nothing to xs:import and nothing to union
      against, and no amount of schema design changes that.

      ⭐⭐ WHAT THIS TYPE DOES INSTEAD, AND IT IS THE ACHIEVABLE THING: it forces the
      sender to NAME THE AUTHORITY. A validator here cannot tell you whether
      `fairValue` is a real IFRS basis, but it can guarantee that the document says
      which body the sender got it from, which makes the claim checkable by a human
      reader, or by a downstream regime-specific validator that DOES know the list.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND THE SLOT IS THE POINT: A TAXONOMY NO QUERY CAN REACH IS NOT A CARRIED
      AUTHORITY. An adopting corpus was found to have recorded a borrowed set's
      authority COMPLETELY AND CORRECTLY: the statutory instrument and its annexes,
      the issuing authority, three artifacts each with a sha256, which of them is the
      law, the page ranges, and an explicit citation rule, in a prose file BESIDE the
      data. NOTHING CAN JOIN ON IT.

      ⛔ A source note beside a table is to this type exactly what a long free-text
      note is to `Absence`: excellent, correct, and unjoinable. The rule is the same
      one stated there, arriving at the authority instead of at the reason.

      ⭐ THAT MAKES THIS TYPE'S CASE STRONGER RATHER THAN WEAKER, and it is worth
      saying to an adopter who feels accused: the authority was known all along and
      there was nowhere to put it. A missing slot is the cheapest kind of adoption gap
      to close. See conformance/adoption.md for what the gap looks like from inside a
      corpus that already has one.

      ⭐ `taxonomy` is xs:anyURI precisely so one mechanism covers both cases: a
      SCHEMA NAMESPACE where the regime publishes one, and a STANDARD'S IDENTIFIER
      where the authority is a document. Neither is privileged.

      ⚠️ WHERE A REGIME DOES PUBLISH AN ENUMERATION (SAF-T's referencial and IES's
      AnexoASNC both look like schema enumerations rather than prose), a conformance
      profile may xs:import it and xs:union against it, tightening validation for
      documents claiming that profile. THAT IS AN ADDITION AND NOT A REDESIGN, and
      it should follow real adopters rather than precede them. See conformance/.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="taxonomy" type="xs:anyURI"/>
      <xs:element name="value"    type="xs:token"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="ForeignId">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      A reference to ONE ELEMENT IN ONE OTHER DOCUMENT (a BPMN task, an ERP
      operation, a workflow node) so that a draw filed here can be lined up with
      the process notation the sender already runs.

      ⛔ DELIBERATELY NOT BorrowedTerm, DESPITE THE IDENTICAL SHAPE. A taxonomy
      defines a VOCABULARY shared by many documents, and its values are members of
      a published set. A notation plus an id names a POSITION IN ONE FILE that no
      authority publishes. Merging them would send a receiver looking for `task_17`
      in a normative list, and finding nothing there would look like a defect in the
      list. Two facts of different kinds, one shape: the shape is not the fact.

      `notation` examples: the BPMN 2.0 namespace URI, or any URI identifying the
      document or system the id belongs to.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="notation" type="xs:anyURI"/>
      <xs:element name="id"       type="xs:token"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       QUANTUM: the indivisible unit a supply comes in.

       ⭐⭐ THE STRUCTURE THE MODEL IS NAMED AFTER. In `a mod n`, `a` is continuous
       demand and `n` is the modulus, the whole unit you must divide by. The
       remainder is what that division leaves, so this type is the CAUSE and
       Remainder is the EFFECT.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="ConstraintOrigin">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔ WHY THIS AXIS EXISTS AT ALL, AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO A QUESTION THAT WAS
      BEING ARGUED AS ONE THING: "is the quantum a property of the supply, or of how
      you buy it?" BOTH KINDS EXIST, THEY BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY, AND MERGING THEM
      DESTROYS A FALSIFIER.

      ⭐ THE TEST IS WHO YOU HAVE TO TALK TO IN ORDER TO CHANGE IT:

        intrinsic    nobody can. One person is one person; a launch is indivisible
                     and you cannot ship two-thirds of an integration. Changing it
                     means a different kind of supply, not a different deal.

        contractual  the counterparty can. A minimum reserved block, a minimum
                     commitment, the size of a funding round. It is somebody else's
                     terms, and terms move.

        policy       WE can, unilaterally. A price ladder's rungs, a hiring unit, a
                     release train. No permission required from anyone.

      ⭐⭐ AND THE SPLIT IS WHAT KEEPS ONE OF THE MODEL'S OWN FALSIFIERS HONEST. The
      falsifier is "show a platform layer whose supply is genuinely continuous". A
      CONTRACTUAL quantum going continuous refutes nothing: cloud services are sold
      in ever finer increments and the market simply moved. Only an INTRINSIC
      quantum going continuous would be a real problem for the model. Without this
      field the two are the same observation and the falsifier is unusable.

      ⚠️ It also ranks the interventions, and not in the flattering order. A policy
      quantum is a RULE, which systems theory puts high on the leverage list; a
      contractual one is a negotiation; an intrinsic one is not a lever at all. The
      quantum most organisations spend their effort on is the one they cannot move.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="intrinsic"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="contractual"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="policy"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <xs:complexType name="LumpyQuantum">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      A supply that only comes in whole units. `size` is the size of ONE unit, in
      the unit of the NAMEPLATE THIS DIVIDES, so that a demand and a quantum can be
      divided.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      ⚠️ THE RULE USED TO SAY "THE UNIT OF THE LAYER'S DEMAND" AND ONE FILING READ IT
      THE OTHER WAY, so it is worth saying which reading is meant and why. Where the
      nameplate is a RATE — `3 launches per quarter`, `10 shifts per week` — it is
      tempting to file the quantum as the bare thing, `launches`, on the reasoning
      that a lump is a lump. ⛔ THAT IS WRONG, AND THE ARGUMENT IS ARITHMETICAL
      RATHER THAN STYLISTIC. The whole purpose of a quantum is that `nameplate / q`
      and `demand / q` are COUNTS. `(launches per quarter) / launches` is not a
      count, it is a frequency, and the decomposition it feeds is meaningless. ⭐ The
      lump on a rate is a lump OF THE RATE: not "one launch" but ONE LAUNCH SLOT PER
      QUARTER, which is the thing you cannot hold two thirds of.

      ⚠️ AND THE RULE KEYS OFF THE NAMEPLATE RATHER THAN THE DEMAND, though in a
      well-formed layer these agree. `size` is nested inside `nameplate/divisibility`
      because it describes how THE SUPPLY comes; a demand is not divided into lumps
      by anybody.

      ⭐ `size` is a Claim rather than a number because a quantum is often itself a
      range ("one to three months' notice", "a hire is four to nine weeks"), and
      because a point value is the degenerate Claim, nothing is lost when it is
      exact. `absent reason="unmeasured"` is the honest filing for a supply known to
      be lumpy whose unit nobody has measured.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="size"   type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="origin" type="pm:ConstraintOrigin"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Continuity">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔ A SUPPLY WITH NO QUANTUM HAS NO REMAINDER, AND SAYING SO IS A LEGITIMATE
      USE OF THIS SCHEMA RATHER THAN AN ADMISSION. The layer is simply outside the
      model's subject.

      ⭐⭐ AND THE EXIT IS NORMALLY PRICED, WHICH IS WHAT `premium` RECORDS: the
      difference per unit between buying continuously and buying in lumps. Managed
      endpoints that bill strictly linearly cost more per unit than reserved
      capacity in blocks, and the gap is what you pay to leave the type.

      ⛔⛔ THIS IS ALSO WHERE THE MODEL CAN BE BEATEN, AND THE FIELD IS HERE SO THAT
      BEATING IT IS A FILING RATHER THAN AN ARGUMENT:

        premium above 0   the priced exit. Consistent with the model.
        premium none      filed as zero by somebody who looked: a continuous supply
                          that costs no more than the lumpy one.
        premium below 0   the continuous option is CHEAPER. That is a real problem for
                          the model, and it should be filed exactly this way rather
                          than discussed.

      ⚠️ `none` and `unmeasured` are not interchangeable here. One is a
      counter-example; the other is a gap.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="premium" type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Divisibility">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      Lumpy or continuous: a choice, because they are different kinds of supply
      rather than one shape with a size that might be zero. A continuous supply does
      not have a quantum of zero; it has no quantum, and the difference is the
      whole model.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND THE CHOICE ANSWERS "DIVIDES BY HOW MUCH", WHICH THIS TYPE NEVER HAD TO
      SAY UNTIL THERE WAS A SECOND AXIS. Read the two as functions of the amount asked
      for and the third one draws itself:

        continuous     a line          any amount is available
        lumpy          a STAIRCASE     q*floor(x/q); the step width is the quantum
        `window`       a SQUARE WAVE   the same staircase, on the TIME axis

      ⭐ A staircase is a square wave in amount; a duty cycle is a staircase in time.
      Same function, different axis — which is why the third belongs with these two and
      why it is NOT a third member of the choice.

      ⛔⛔ IT IS A SECOND AXIS AND A CHOICE WOULD FORCE A FALSE PICK. Eight-GPU nodes
      available only between 02:00 and 05:00 are lumpy in amount AND intermittent in
      time, and that is an ordinary supply rather than an exotic one. So `window` is a
      SEQUENCE member beside the choice rather than a third branch of it.

      ⚠️ IT WAS OPTIONAL AND IS NOW A REQUIRED `StatedLumpyQuantum`, which is a breaking
      change made on purpose. Optional bought backward compatibility for documents
      written before the element existed; what it cost was the difference between a
      supply that runs continuously and a supply nobody asked about — and, worse, a
      third state this very annotation describes two paragraphs down as MALFORMED. See
      StatedLumpyQuantum.

      ⭐⭐ THE MACHINERY IS ALREADY HERE AND SO IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE STATEMENT.
      A time lump is a `LumpyQuantum` — a size with a unit, and an origin saying who
      sets it. The CYCLE comes from `Claim`'s rule that a unit's denominator must cover
      at least one whole duty cycle, so

        nameplate  2160 muffins per day  +  window size  3 hours

      is complete: the denominator gives the period, the window gives the part of it the
      supply exists in. The denominator rule and this element are one fact from two sides.

      ⚠️ AND WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT REACH. `Divisibility` sits on `Nameplate`,
      so `window` is a fact about SUPPLY. The mirror — demand with a lifetime, an SLA
      that says how long a queued order stays an order — is a fact about DEMAND, and
      `Layer/timeSlack` already records where that has to go: "if demand ever gains
      structure this is the first thing that should move into it." Half the axis, filed
      as half rather than pretended whole.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:choice>
        <xs:element name="lumpy"      type="pm:LumpyQuantum"/>
        <xs:element name="continuous" type="pm:Continuity"/>
      </xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="window" type="pm:StatedLumpyQuantum">
        <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
          THE SUPPLY EXISTS ONLY IN WINDOWS OF THIS SIZE, one per period of the
          nameplate's unit. A line running 02:00-05:00 files `3 hours` against a
          nameplate quoted per day; a machine stopped two hours a day files `22 hours`.

          ⭐⭐⭐ THIS ELEMENT IS A NOTE ON THE UNIT'S DENOMINATOR, AND THE TWO ARE ONE
          FACT. The denominator supplies the PERIOD; the window supplies the LIVE PART
          of it; the ratio is the duty fraction:

            nameplate  2160 muffins per day     the denominator: one day
            window     3 hours                  the live part of it
                                                3h / 1 day  =  the duty fraction

          ⭐ WHICH IS WHY THE DENOMINATOR RULE AT `Claim` IS ITS PRECONDITION rather than
          a separate convention. "The denominator must cover at least one whole duty
          cycle" exists so that the window has a well-defined thing to be a fraction OF.
          Quote the same line `720 muffins per hour` and the window is longer than its own
          period, which is not a tight filing but an incoherent one.

          ⛔⛔ SO A WINDOW IS MALFORMED ON A UNIT WITH NO DENOMINATOR, AND THAT IS
          `notApplicable` RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. `12 people` has no period, so "5 days"
          has nothing to be five days of; `GPU-hour` carries its hour in the NUMERATOR and
          is a quantity of resource-time rather than a rate. Both are stocks, and a stock
          has no cycle to be live in part of. ⭐ `notApplicable`'s own gloss is the reason
          this is not pedantry: "a receiver that treats this as a gap will report a
          deficiency that does not exist", and a receiver auditing duty cycles across a
          corpus of headcount layers would report twenty of them.
          ⭐ THAT IS ALSO WHY A FILED QUANTUM IS RARE RATHER THAN THE ELEMENT BEING
          UNDER-USED. Of the units in this corpus only `shifts per week`, `turnos por
          semana` and `launches per quarter` have a denominator at all, and only the
          shared line has a real duty cycle inside one.
          ⛔ THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO END "a supply that is always on does not need saying
          so", AND THAT WAS THE DEFECT. It is exactly what needs saying: `absent
          reason="none"` is the always-on filing, and everything above about stocks
          having no cycle is `notApplicable`. Rare in its VALUE, universal in its
          ELEMENT.
          ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. A unit is an `xs:token`, so nothing can tell a
          rate from a stock, or check a duration against a period written in free text.

          ⛔ SIZE IS THE ON-DURATION, NOT THE GAP. Both readings are natural and they
          are complements, so a filing that means one and is read as the other is out by
          the whole cycle. The on-duration is the one that multiplies: nameplate rate
          times window over period is what the supply actually delivers.
          ⛔ A WINDOW IS CARRIED THROUGH A FUSION AND NEVER SUMMED. Two members naming
          one machine file one calendar between them; summing gives ten days a week. It
          is a property rather than a quantity, which is why `EliminationAgainst` has
          three members and no fourth — there is nothing to eliminate.
          ⛔ NEITHER IS REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 cannot compare a size against
          the period in another element's unit, and cannot see across two documents at
          all. An implementer still owes both.

          ⭐ `origin` IS NOT DECORATION HERE AND IT SEPARATES TWO THINGS THAT LOOK
          IDENTICAL IN THE NUMBER. A maintenance stop is `intrinsic` — the machine needs
          it and no one may decide otherwise. A shift pattern is `policy` — somebody
          chose 02:00 and somebody could add a second shift. Both cut the daily figure
          the same way; only one is a lever.

          ⚠️ A WINDOW IS WHY A DURATION CANNOT BE DERIVED FROM A CLEARANCE. `q / clearance`
          assumes the spare is spread evenly across the denominator, and this element is
          the statement that it is not. Where a quantum is filed, `Layer/timeSlack` must
          not be `absent reason="derived"`.
          ⭐ AND THE WRAPPER EXTENDS THE RULE TO THE CASE IT COULD NOT REACH. Under
          `absent reason="unmeasured"` nobody knows whether the spare is spread evenly, so
          a derived time slack is an ASSUMPTION wearing a computation's clothes and the
          same prohibition applies. Only `none` and `notApplicable` license the derivation.
          Two of the four states permit it, and before the wrapper all four looked alike.
          ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot compare
          across elements. An implementer still owes this check.
        </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       FACILITY: one supply, with BOTH faces at once.

       ⛔ RESTRUCTURED WHEN THE FLOW HALF LANDED. The first draft had a `face`
       discriminator and one `amount`, so a document carried two elements for one
       supply with nothing linking them, while the annotation next to it said the
       two faces are a PRODUCT, NOT A CHOICE. The structure now says what the prose
       said, and the `Face` enum is gone: a discriminator naming which of two
       positions you are in is redundant once there are two positions.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Nameplate">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      THE DECLARED FACE: what was committed, rated, reserved or bought. Somebody
      CHOSE it.

      ⛔ IT HAS NO MEASUREMENT BASIS ELEMENT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. A declared
      capacity is not a measurement, so asking for its basis is a CATEGORY ERROR
      rather than a gap, and the position already says the number is declared.
      (The `nameplate` basis value still exists, for a declared number filed in the
      MEASURED face, which is the case that actually misleads.)

      `capacitySlack` is the one fact about a supply that no arithmetic can recover:
      how far the draw may exceed the rating. A GPU cannot run at 110 per cent; a disk
      cannot hold more than it holds; a month has at most 31 days — all of them file
      `absent reason="none"`. A PERSON CAN, and in most stacks labour is the only
      supply that can.

      ⭐⭐ That one figure is the missing half of an existing costing method.
      Time-Driven ABC reports unused capacity as a separate figure, a good idea for
      exactly this model's reason, but implements ONE SIGN, because its unit of
      analysis is a machine-hour and a machine cannot run hot. Change the unit to a
      person and the account becomes signed. The negative branch has no home in any
      standard cost system, and this flag is where a document says the branch is
      inhabited.

      ⭐⭐⭐ `amountOrigin` IS WHO YOU HAVE TO TALK TO IN ORDER TO HOLD A DIFFERENT
      NUMBER OF UNITS, AND IT IS NOT THE QUESTION `LumpyQuantum/origin` ANSWERS.
      That one asks who sets the size of ONE unit. This one asks who sets HOW MANY of
      them you hold. A vendor fixes the block at eight GPU, which is a CONTRACTUAL
      size; your budget fixes you at one block, which is a POLICY amount. Two
      constraints, two different authors, and an enumeration answering only the first
      leaves the second unrecorded.

      ⛔ IT IS WHAT MAKES THE REMAINDER'S DECOMPOSITION FALSIFIABLE. A remainder
      separates into whole quanta plus a residue, and the whole-quanta part is a
      DECISION while the residue is not (see Remainder). Without this element,
      "management chose to run three launches short" and "management could not have
      run anything else" are the same document, and the model's own conservation claim
      cannot be tested against either.

      ⚠️ AND IT SURFACES A MISSING COUPLING. `intrinsic` filed on a layer whose count
      is actually bounded by ANOTHER LAYER is not an intrinsic constraint at all. It
      is a dependence, and it belongs in `Coupling` with the observation that produced
      it. A sender reaching for `intrinsic` here should rule that out first.

      ⚠️ DIRECTION IS DELIBERATELY NOT SPLIT. Who can let you hold MORE and who can
      let you hold LESS can genuinely differ, since a commitment may be raisable at
      will and lowerable only by agreement. It is one element until an example needs
      two, and that example should be filed rather than anticipated.

      ⭐⭐⭐ `capacitySlack` AND `inventorySlack` ARE TWO OF THREE, AND TREATING THE
      FIRST AS A ONE-OFF WAS AN ERROR THIS SCHEMA MADE FOR SEVERAL DRAFTS. Each of
      Factory Physics' three buffers has a SLACK — how much of a remainder it can
      take before it is exhausted — and for several drafts only one of them was
      written down at all:

        capacity    the supply runs above its rating   Nameplate/capacitySlack
        inventory   the output is held ahead           Nameplate/inventorySlack
        time        the demand survives being held     Layer/timeSlack

      ⭐⭐ EACH IS NAMED FOR THE BUFFER IT GATES, WHICH IS WHY THE RULE FITS ON ONE
      LINE. `absorber` names a buffer; the slack of that same buffer bounds what may
      be attributed to it. Two of these were previously called `admitsInterference`
      and `admitsDelay`, which gated `capacity` and `time` under different words, so
      every statement of the rule needed a three-row lookup table first.

      ⛔⛔⛔ AND THEY WERE BOOLEANS, WHICH IS WORSE THAN IMPRECISE — IT IS UNCHECKABLE.
      A boolean says a buffer is available; it cannot say AVAILABLE, BARELY. Take a
      conveyor at a hundred slots an hour carrying ninety-five muffins: five slots an
      hour are free, so a late muffin waits about twelve minutes for one and no
      longer. The belt admits delay — truthfully — and a sender with an hour of
      lateness could attribute the whole of it to the time buffer and file `true`
      without lying about anything. ⛔ SO THE BOOLEAN CAUGHT A CONTRADICTION AND NEVER
      AN ATTRIBUTION: it caught "you said inventory absorbed it and also said this
      cannot be stored", and never "you said inventory absorbed six months of output
      through a two-day shelf life." The comfortable exits stayed free, one level
      down, which is the exact defect adding the conditions was meant to close.

      ⭐ THE MODEL HAD ALREADY MADE THIS ARGUMENT ONE TYPE OVER. `LumpyQuantum/size`
      is a Claim rather than a number "because a point value is the degenerate Claim,
      nothing is lost when it is exact." A slack is the same case, and the old
      `StatedBoolean` — a type that existed for these three fields and nothing else
      in either schema, while `StatedClaim` carried every other quantity — was that
      discipline abandoned at precisely the three facts that decide whether this
      model's own thesis survives.

      ⭐⭐ TYPED ABSENCE THEN SUPPLIES A THIRD STATE THE BOOLEAN COULD NOT, and it is
      the state most senders are actually in:

        a claim                     available up to this much, in the layer's unit
        absent reason="none"        somebody looked and it is zero — the old `false`
        absent reason="unmeasured"  available, and nobody has sized it
        absent reason="derived"     computable here, e.g. from the clearance

      The old `true` was doing the second and third jobs at once and could not tell
      a reader which it meant.

      ⛔ A HOLDER'S SHARE MUST NOT EXCEED THE SLACK OF THE BUFFER ITS `absorber`
      NAMES. An unmeasured slack SUSPENDS that check rather than passing it, exactly
      as an unmeasured share suspends the share sum.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.

      ⛔ AND DO NOT INVENT A SLACK TO FILL THE FIELD. A fabricated number wears more
      authority than the boolean ever did, and `unmeasured` is a complete answer.

      ⛔⛔ A SIZED SLACK SHOULD SAY WHO SET IT, AND ALL THREE ARE THE ONLY CONSTRAINTS
      IN THIS MODEL THAT DO NOT. `Nameplate/amountOrigin` says who can hold a different
      number of units; `LumpyQuantum/origin` says who sets the size of one; a slack says
      how much room a buffer has and NOTHING about whether anybody can move it. The slot
      already exists — `Claim/boundOrigin` — and across the whole corpus it is filed once.

        intrinsic     a shelf life, a material limit. Not a lever at all
        contractual   an SLA that gives queued demand a lifetime; a reserved block.
                      A negotiation
        policy        a queue depth or an overtime ceiling somebody here chose.
                      Ours to change unilaterally

      ⭐ THE SLA CASE IS WHY THIS IS NOT BOOKKEEPING. Two layers can file the same
      `timeSlack` and mean opposite things: one is how long the work physically keeps,
      the other is how long a contract says the customer waits. The first is a fact
      about the world and the second is a term somebody negotiated, and a reader
      deciding where to push cannot tell them apart from the number.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. `boundOrigin` is optional on every Claim, and
      XSD 1.0 cannot require it here and not elsewhere.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="amount"         type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="amountOrigin"   type="pm:StatedConstraintOrigin"/>
      <xs:element name="divisibility"   type="pm:StatedDivisibility"/>
      <xs:element name="capacitySlack"  type="pm:StatedClaim">
        <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
          HOW FAR THE SUPPLY CAN RUN ABOVE ITS RATING, in the layer's unit.

          ⚠️ NOT SPARE CAPACITY, AND THE IDIOM POINTS THE WRONG WAY. "Capacity slack"
          in ordinary speech means the UNUSED part of a rating, which in this model is
          the clearance and is already carried by `Remainder`. This is the opposite
          end: the room ABOVE the nameplate, which is what the capacity buffer draws
          on when it absorbs a remainder. A layer can have plenty of the first and
          none of the second.

          ⭐ PERISHABILITY DOES NOT APPLY HERE, WHICH IS WHY THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT.
          Holding spare is always available and merely costs money, so only the
          negative direction ever needed a figure. Three buffers, three slacks, and
          this one measures half an axis. Said rather than pretended away.

          ⛔ RUNNING ABOVE A RATING IS INTRINSICALLY A RATE CONCEPT, AND ON A LAYER
          WHOSE UNIT IS A STOCK THAT IS A TRAP. You cannot exceed a stock: twelve people
          is twelve people, and there is no thirteenth to draw on. What you exceed is a
          RATE — twelve people at fifty hours instead of forty. `enterprise-contract`'s
          filer felt this and wrote it into the note: "HOW FAR ABOVE, AND FOR HOW LONG,
          nobody here has measured." Two dimensions named, one field to hold them.
          ⭐ SO THE MEASUREMENT IS A DURATION AND THE FILING IS A QUANTITY, exactly as
          at `Layer/timeSlack`, and the bridge is the same: `quantity = duration x rate`.
          Cross it, and say in the note that you did.

          ⭐⭐⭐ THIS FIELD HAS A SECOND JOB, AND IT IS THE ONLY PLACE THE MODEL
          MEASURES SOMETHING WITH NO INSTRUMENT BEHIND IT. Everywhere else a slack
          BOUNDS shares that were already filed. Here it closes an equation over
          quantities a filer had to supply anyway. Start from the exposure defined at
          Fit, which comes from `demand` and `nameplate` and needs nothing else:

            exposure = max(0, demand.high - nameplate.low)

          That is how far demand can run past the supply at the worst corner. None of
          it can be absorbed beyond this slack, so whatever is left went unserved, and
          unserved demand is `customer` or `unrealised`:

            exposure  ≤  capacitySlack.high + SUM(customer, unrealised share highs)

          ⭐ IN WORDS: what your own numbers say could have gone wrong is at most what
          you can absorb plus what you admit went unserved. Four filed quantities,
          no instrument, and the SHORTFALL IS THE INTERESTING NUMBER — the part of a
          remainder that happened and that nothing recorded, which is the whole of
          this model's subject stated as arithmetic instead of as an argument.

          ⚠️ EVALUATED AT ONE CORNER, for the reason given at Fit: the clearance and
          interference sides are anti-correlated, so anything summed across the demand
          range reports a state that occurs in no week. Everything above is the high
          corner and nothing else.

          ⚠️ AND IT SAYS SOMETHING NEW ONLY UNDER `transition`. Under `interference`
          the exposure IS the remainder magnitude's high bound, so the inequality
          degenerates into the sum rule already stated at Holder. Under `clearance` the
          exposure is zero and there is nothing to say. It is a transition-fit
          instrument, recovering exactly what the magnitude loses in the one case where
          the magnitude is lossy.

          ⛔ IT IS ALSO UNEXERCISED, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. Not one layer in
          `assets/corpus/` files a NUMERIC `capacitySlack`; every one is `none` or
          `unmeasured`, which makes this check silent on nineteen of twenty-one layers.
          A rule with nothing to bound passes loudest, and the cure is a filer
          measuring how far a supply can run hot — not a figure invented to give the
          rule something to chew on.
          ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
          compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
          cannot take it from them.
        </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
      <xs:element name="inventorySlack" type="pm:StatedClaim">
        <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
          HOW MUCH OUTPUT CAN BE HELD AHEAD, in the layer's unit. Supply
          perishability, and the exact mirror of `Layer/timeSlack`.

          ⭐ THE SHARPEST CASE IS LABOUR AND IT IS WHY THIS IS NOT DECORATIVE.
          Capacity not used today is GONE: last week's unused hours cannot be
          stockpiled to serve this week's demand. `absorber = inventory` on a labour
          layer is therefore flatly false, obvious to any reader, and was entirely
          unreachable by any check until this element existed. The filing is
          `absent reason="none"` — somebody looked, and the answer is zero.

          ⚠️ AND THE SAME DIMENSION TRAP AS THE OTHER TWO. What a stockpile is naturally
          measured in is a DURATION — how long the shelf life is, how many weeks of cover
          the floor holds — and what this field takes is a QUANTITY in the layer's unit,
          because it is compared against holder shares. `enterprise-contract`'s
          `capability` shows the conversion done correctly and silently: "finished and
          unshipped for up to about TWO QUARTERS' worth of launches" is a duration, and at
          roughly three launches a quarter it was filed as `[2, 4, 6] launches per
          quarter`. ⭐ THE MULTIPLICATION IS OWED AND IT IS INVISIBLE ONCE DONE, so the
          note is the only place a reader can see whether it happened.
        </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
      </xs:element>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Jagged">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      THE OPERATIONAL FACE: what actually happened. Nobody chose it.

      `draw` is what the supply actually served, which is NOT what was demanded and
      NOT what was committed. Demand that went unserved never became a draw at all, so
      a model that keeps only the draw has silently discarded every customer it never
      served.

      ⭐ Its job in this model is narrow and it is not to size the remainder: the
      draw is what says WHO HELD IT. Sitting exactly on the nameplate under excess
      demand means the excess went unserved; sitting above it means somebody absorbed
      the excess. See Fit.

      `measurementBasis` belongs HERE and not on the nameplate, because this face is
      the one that claims to be a measurement. ⚠️ Filing `contributed = nameplate`
      here is the honest way to admit that a "measured" figure was read off a rate
      card.

      ⚠️ NO PERIOD ELEMENT, AND THAT IS A POSITION RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT: time is
      an axis on a SIMULATION of this model, not a field on its types. A draw over
      an interval is expressed by making the interval part of the unit ("hours per
      month"), and `asOf` on the Claim says when the assertion was true. A document
      set that needs real intervals should say so, and it would be a schema change
      argued on evidence rather than assumed here.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="draw"             type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="measurementBasis" type="pm:StatedBasis"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Facility">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      One supply, both faces. A facility has a nameplate and a jagged face AT ONCE,
      a product and not a choice, and either face's leaf quantities may be typed
      absences, so a facility nobody has measured is still expressible without
      silence.

      ⛔ THE REMAINDER IS NOT HERE ANY MORE, AND THE MOVE IS A FINDING. It used to
      hang off the facility, which asserts that a remainder is a property of the
      SUPPLY. It is not: you cannot compute one without the demand, and the demand
      belongs to the layer where supply and demand meet. See Layer.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="label"     type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="nameplate" type="pm:Nameplate"/>
      <xs:element name="jagged"    type="pm:Jagged"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       THE LAYER STACK: where a continuous demand meets a quantized supply.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Layer">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐⭐ WHAT MAKES SOMETHING A LAYER, STATED AS A TEST RATHER THAN A LIST:

      > A LAYER IS A PLACE WHERE A REMAINDER CAN BE HELD INDEPENDENTLY OF EVERY
      > OTHER LAYER.

      ⭐ The definition and the model's fourth falsifier are the SAME SENTENCE: if
      two candidate layers' remainders always move together, they were never two
      layers. That is what makes this a test you can fail rather than a taxonomy you
      have to accept, and it is why this schema DOES NOT ENUMERATE THE LAYERS.
      Compute, storage, labour, capability, capital and price are what one stack
      looked like when it was written down, not a closed set.

      THE THREE QUANTITIES, AND KEEPING THEM APART IS THE ENTIRE POINT:

        demand              what was ASKED. Continuous, and usually a genuine range.
        supply.nameplate    what was COMMITTED. Quantized: it comes in whole units.
        supply.jagged.draw  what was actually SERVED.

      ⛔ AN ERP KEEPS ONE OF THESE AND MOST DASHBOARDS KEEP TWO, and the third is
      never the spare one. The remainder is nameplate minus demand; THE DRAW IS WHAT
      SAYS WHO HELD IT. Saturated supply and absorbed excess look identical in any
      system that stores the served figure and the committed one and never asks what
      was asked. See Fit.

      `remainder` is REQUIRED, as a StatedRemainder, so that "there is none here"
      is a filed claim rather than an empty field. That requirement is the model
      asserting itself in a form somebody can contradict.

      ⭐⭐ `timeSlack` IS THE THIRD BUFFER'S SLACK: HOW MUCH DEMAND SURVIVES BEING
      HELD. It completes the set begun at Nameplate/capacitySlack and
      Nameplate/inventorySlack, and without it a claim that `time` absorbed a
      remainder is unfalsifiable.

      ⛔⛔ IT IS DEMAND PERISHABILITY AND IT IS NOT A QUESTION ABOUT WILLINGNESS.
      An earlier draft of this annotation asked "will this demand wait?", and that
      wording was a defect rather than a shorthand. ⭐ THE HOLDER DOES NOT GET TO
      REFUSE. A customer who leaves has not declined anything; the demand DECAYED,
      the same way an unsold pastry decays, and the same way `inventorySlack` measures
      supply decaying. Asking instead what a third party is WILLING to tolerate puts
      an unfalsifiable claim about somebody else's state of mind into a filing made by
      the party who benefits from the answer — which is the unchecked exit that
      terminates inquiry, reintroduced in the very element added to close it.

      ⭐ SO THE PAIR IS EXACT. `inventorySlack` asks whether the SUPPLY survives being
      held across time; this asks whether the DEMAND does. There is no fourth
      condition because demand has no nameplate to run hot against.

      ⭐⭐ AND IT IS OBSERVABLE, WHICH WILLINGNESS WAS NOT. Whether queued work is
      eventually served or quietly ages out is visible in any ticket system.

      ⛔⛔ BUT WHAT YOU OBSERVE IS A DURATION AND WHAT THIS FIELD HOLDS IS A QUANTITY,
      AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS PARAGRAPH FILED THE WRONG ONE. It said: on a belt at
      a hundred slots an hour carrying ninety-five muffins, five slots an hour are free,
      "so a late muffin waits about twelve minutes" — and twelve minutes is not what
      goes here. The slack is FIVE MUFFINS AN HOUR. Twelve minutes is `q / clearance`,
      a different quantity in a different dimension that happens to describe the same
      belt.
      ⭐ THE BRIDGE IS `quantity = duration x rate`, and it must be crossed by the
      filer, because this field is compared against holder shares and shares are in the
      layer's unit. A duration filed here is dimensionally wrong however true it is.
      ⚠️ AND THE PULL TOWARD THE DURATION IS NOT SLOPPINESS. A buffer's size is
      naturally measured as a duration — how long the work sits, how long before the
      caller leaves — so every slack in this schema will be MEASURED one way and FILED
      another. Say which you did.
      ⛔ A SLACK IS EXPRESSED IN THE UNIT OF THE SHARES IT BOUNDS, and a slack measured
      as a duration is converted before filing.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. A unit is an `xs:token`; XSD 1.0 cannot compare
      one element's unit against another's, and no schema can see a multiplication that
      was skipped. An implementer still owes both checks.

      ⛔⛔ `derived` IS AVAILABLE ONLY WHERE THE LAYER RUNS CONTINUOUSLY, which almost
      nothing does. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR: nothing in a document says what duty
      cycle its supply runs on, so a `derived` slack cannot be checked for the assumption
      it rests on. The derivation `duration = q / clearance` assumes the clearance is
      spread evenly across the unit's denominator, and a duty cycle is exactly the
      violation of that assumption. A line running 02:00-05:00 at one muffin every five
      seconds:

        nameplate   10800 s / 5 s      =  2160 muffins per day
        demand                            2000 muffins per day
        clearance                          160 muffins per day   ** THIS is the slack **

        spread over the day  1440 min / 160  =  9 minutes
        inside the window     180 min / 160  =  68 seconds
        missed the window       05:00-02:00  =  21 hours

      Nine minutes occurs NOWHERE on that line. It is an average of two numbers, neither
      of which is nine minutes. The quantity — 160 a day — survives the duty cycle
      untouched, which is the second reason this field holds the quantity.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND HENCE A FILING RULE THIS CORPUS ALREADY OBEYS WITHOUT HAVING WRITTEN IT
      DOWN: THE UNIT'S DENOMINATOR MUST COVER AT LEAST ONE WHOLE DUTY CYCLE. "2160
      muffins per day" is true always; "720 muffins per hour" is true for three hours
      and false for twenty-one. `shifts per week` and `launches per quarter` both pass.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR: a unit is an `xs:token` and nothing in it says
      what cycle the supply runs on.

      ⛔ IT SITS ON THE LAYER RATHER THAN ON THE SUPPLY BECAUSE IT IS A FACT ABOUT
      DEMAND, and `demand` is a bare Claim with nowhere to hang it. If demand ever
      gains structure this is the first thing that should move into it.

      ⛔ A HOLDER'S SHARE MUST NOT EXCEED THIS WHERE `absorber = time`.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and cannot
      compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema
      cannot take it from them.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="name"        type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="demand"      type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="timeSlack"   type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="supply"      type="pm:Facility"/>
      <xs:element name="remainder"   type="pm:StatedRemainder"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Coupling">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ AN OBSERVED DEPENDENCE BETWEEN TWO LAYERS' REMAINDERS, WHICH IS TO SAY,
      THIS ELEMENT EXISTS SO THAT THE MODEL CAN BE REFUTED IN ITS OWN FORMAT.

      The stack is assumed to be a set of INDEPENDENT quantizations, and that
      assumption has never been tested. If relieving one layer's constraint
      measurably reduces another layer's remainder, the layers move together, the
      single-constraint schools of thought have far more purchase here than this
      model allows, and the multi-layer framing is the weaker description.

      ⭐ ZERO COUPLINGS IS THE MODEL'S ASSUMPTION, NOT A PROVEN PROPERTY. A document
      with no couplings is not evidence of independence; it is a document where
      nobody looked.
      ⭐⭐ THAT SENTENCE STOOD HERE FOR TWO REVISIONS WHILE THE ELEMENT IT DESCRIBES
      WAS `minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"`, WHICH IS TO SAY THE SCHEMA NAMED THE
      DEFECT AND THEN ENCODED IT. `Stack/couplings` is a `StatedCouplings` now, so
      "nobody looked" and "somebody looked and the layers are independent" are two
      filings rather than one empty list. See StatedCouplings.

      ⭐⭐ AND IT IS WHY THE STACK CARRIES NO ORDER. "Stack" is the inherited word,
      but nothing here claims layer A sits above layer B, because an ordering
      relation between layers WOULD BE a coupling, and would belong in this element
      with its evidence rather than in the sequence of the document. Document order
      carries no meaning.

      `observed` is REQUIRED. A coupling filed without saying what was observed is
      an opinion, and the one element in this schema whose purpose is to carry a
      refutation is the last place to accept one.

      ⭐⭐⭐ WHAT HAPPENS TO A COUPLING WHEN A HIGHER LEVEL FUSES ITS ENDS. A consolidation
      (`asrt:composition`) takes layers from filings below and states its own stack, so a
      coupling filed at one level meets a fusion at the next. Three rules, and none of them
      is reachable by a validator because the two levels are two documents.

      ⛔⛔ FIRST, AND IT IS THE ONE MOST LIKELY TO BE GOT WRONG: A COUPLING IS NEVER
      EVIDENCE FOR A FUSION. Coupling and fungibility are INDEPENDENT AXES, and both
      off-diagonal cases are ordinary. Two delivery teams in two countries are one layer
      and not coupled at all — neither one's remainder moves when the other hires. A
      delivery team and an out-of-hours rota are tightly coupled and NOT one layer, because
      an extra engineer on the rota delivers no features. ⚠️ A fusion that cited a coupling
      as its justification would be answering the wrong question: fungibility is whether a
      unit of supply in one part can serve demand in the other, which is not what a coupling
      measures.

      ⭐⭐ SECOND, A COUPLING PROPAGATES UPWARD AND ATTENUATES. If A and B are coupled and a
      higher level puts A inside X while B stays inside Y, then X and Y are coupled — but
      MORE WEAKLY, because relief applied to X may land on A's fellow parts rather than on
      A. The upper strength is bounded by A's share of X. ⛔ AN INHERITED COUPLING RE-FILED
      AT THE SAME STRENGTH IS EITHER LUCKY OR UNEXAMINED, and `strength` is where that shows.

      ⭐⭐ THIRD, FUSING TWO COUPLED LAYERS DESTROYS A FILED OBSERVATION, AND THE COMPOSER
      OWES A REASON. The coupling had two ends in a stack; above the fusion there is one
      layer and no coupling to file. That is not an omission and not evidence either way —
      it is an observation the upper level has chosen to absorb, and `asrt:Fusion/observed`
      is where it says so. ⚠️ A composition that silently fuses two layers a filing below it
      reported as coupled has overruled that filing without arguing with it.

      ⚠️ AND A COUPLING IS NEVER INHERITED BY COPYING. Each level observed its own system;
      carrying a lower level's row upward unchanged is the cached-value problem `Holder`
      already names, one document apart.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR, for all four: the levels are separate filings.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="from"     type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="to"       type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="observed" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="strength" type="pm:StatedClaim" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Stack">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      The layers of one system THAT MATTERED TO WHOEVER FILED, and what somebody found when
      they looked for couplings between them. ⚠️ Document order is not significant. See Coupling.

      ⛔⛔⛔ THIS TYPE USED TO SAY "the layers of one system" AND READ AS THOUGH IT ENUMERATED
      ONE. It never did. There is one system; a filing holds a PROJECTION of it, and which
      layers are in scope is decided by the reader rather than by the system — which is where
      the analogy to a Rust trait breaks, because there the type decides what it implements.

      ⭐⭐ THE CONSEQUENCE IS WHY `scope` IS REQUIRED. A second document holding a layer this
      one does not is TWO PROJECTIONS OF ONE SYSTEM, not evidence that this filer omitted
      anything. An auditor who composes a client's filing and adds a layer is filing their own
      view, and without a declared scope that reads as an allegation. See ScopeExtent.

      ⛔ `couplings` IS REQUIRED, AND IT IS THE ONLY REQUIRED ELEMENT IN THIS SCHEMA THAT
      ASKS A FILER WHETHER THEY TESTED THE MODEL. Everything else asks what they measured.
      A stack is a claim that these layers hold their remainders independently; requiring
      the wrapper makes the filer say whether that claim was checked, in the same way
      `StatedRemainder` makes them say whether a layer has a remainder at all.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="scope"     type="pm:StatedScope"/>
      <xs:element name="layer"     type="pm:Layer" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="couplings" type="pm:StatedCouplings"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       THE FLOW: what an operation takes, and what it commits somewhere else.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Draw">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      WHAT AN OPERATION TAKES FROM A LAYER, NOW.

      ⭐⭐ THIS IS THE ELEMENT THE WHOLE TYPED-ABSENCE APPARATUS WAS BUILT FOR. The
      absorbed evening, the interruption, the fix that never became a ticket: a draw
      on the labour layer with `quantity` filed as `absent reason="unmeasured"`. It
      is a real draw, it is attributed to a real operation, and it has no number,
      and every one of those three facts survives transmission.

      ⛔ A shortfall with no operation in it produces no signal at all. That is what a
      operation is FOR here: not sequencing, but making a draw ATTRIBUTABLE. An
      unattributed remainder is a number in a report that nobody can act on.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="layer"    type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="quantity" type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Induction">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ A COMMITMENT MADE HERE THAT BECOMES A DRAW SOMEWHERE ELSE: the edge the
      model did not have, and the reason the flow half is not just a list of draws:

      > A NAMEPLATE DECISION IN ONE LAYER BECOMES A JAGGED DRAW IN ANOTHER.

      Selling an offering commits labour that was not previously committed. Standing
      up a service commits operations. Launching a campaign commits build. In every
      case one party decides and a DIFFERENT layer bears it, and NOTHING IN THE
      ACCOUNTS RECORDS THE TRANSFER.

      ⛔ DELIBERATELY NOT THE SAME TYPE AS Draw, DESPITE THE NEARLY IDENTICAL SHAPE.
      A draw is consumption that has happened against a supply; an induction is a
      commitment that CREATES a future draw on a different supply. Folding them into
      one type with a discriminator would put two kinds of fact in one slot, which is
      the error this schema is otherwise built to avoid.

      `decidedBy` names WHO made the commitment. It is optional because a sender may
      genuinely not know, but an induction without it records that the transfer
      happened and not who made it, which is the weaker half of the finding, and a
      receiver is entitled to say so.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="layer"      type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="commitment" type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="decidedBy"  type="xs:string" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Operation">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      THE UNIT AT WHICH A DRAW IS ATTRIBUTABLE.

      ⭐⭐ THE NAME WAS `Step` UNTIL 2026-08-30 AND THE CHANGE IS NOT COSMETIC. A
      STEP IS BY DEFINITION ONE OF A SERIES: the word carries the ordering this
      type explicitly does not have, so it promised a predecessor and a successor
      that are not here and never will be. AN OPERATION IS THE CLASSICAL UNIT OF
      WORK AT A WORK CENTRE: it consumes a resource for a duration, and the ROUTING
      is what puts operations in order. That is precisely this model's split (the
      draws here, the ordering in the process notation), and the manufacturing
      literature drew it first, which makes the borrowing consistent with `absorber`
      and with Fit rather than a fourth vocabulary.

      ⚠️ It was also the wrong side of a boundary. `step` is the incumbent word in
      the platform this model was extracted from, where it names a unit of
      INTERACTION (a record of what a system did), and this type describes the
      world rather than anybody's record of it.

      ⛔ NOT A UNIT OF SEQUENCE. There is no predecessor, no successor, no gateway
      and no condition here, and their absence is a scoping decision rather than an
      omission: BPMN 2.0 models all of that, is an OMG standard with published
      schemas, and restating it inside this namespace would fork it. `foreignId`
      exists so that an operation here can name the same operation THERE.

      ⭐ AN OPERATION CONSUMES AND IT COMMITS, AND THE TWO ARE NOT SYMMETRIC. What it
      consumes is a `draw` against a layer's supply. What it produces is not an
      output quantity; it is a commitment INDUCED on another layer. A model with a
      symmetric input/output pair would render both as flow and lose the asymmetry
      that makes the cross-layer transfer visible.

      ⚠️ At least one draw or induction is REQUIRED. An operation that neither takes
      anything nor commits anything is invisible to this model, and filing one is
      almost always a sign that the process notation was transcribed instead of the
      draws being asked for.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="label"     type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="foreignId" type="pm:ForeignId" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:choice maxOccurs="unbounded">
        <xs:element name="draw"    type="pm:Draw"/>
        <xs:element name="induces" type="pm:Induction"/>
      </xs:choice>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       REGIME: what the document reports under, declared once at the top.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Regime">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ A REGIME IS THREE AXES AND THE COMMONEST MISTAKE IS TO WRITE THEM AS ONE
      LIST. "us-gaap · us-accrual · pt" looks like three values of one thing and is
      not: the first is a FRAMEWORK, the second a BASIS OF ACCOUNTING, the third a
      JURISDICTION. A list mixing them cannot answer any of the three questions.

        jurisdiction  WHERE. Absent for a framework that is not a country's. IFRS
                      has no jurisdiction, and forcing one invents a fact.
        framework     WHAT the entity reports under, AS CODED BY A NAMED AUTHORITY,
                      which is why it is a BorrowedTerm and not a token.
        the taxonomy  WHO CODES IT, carried inside that term.
        inside it

      ⭐⭐ THE THIRD AXIS IS THE ONE THAT SURPRISES PEOPLE, AND PORTUGAL IS THE PROOF.
      THE SAME ENTITY UNDER THE SAME FRAMEWORK HAS DIFFERENT CODES IN DIFFERENT
      AUTHORITIES' TAXONOMIES, and the two published four-value lists do not line up:

        SAF-T PT referencial   S · M · N · O
        IES AnexoASNC          NIC · NCRF · NCRF-PE · NC-ME

      NCRF and NCRF-PE BOTH collapse into S, and O has no AnexoASNC counterpart. So
      `S` does not identify a framework and `NCRF-PE` is not a SAF-T value. A code
      without its authority is genuinely ambiguous rather than merely unattributed,
      which is the whole reason BorrowedTerm requires the taxonomy.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND THEREFORE: DECLARING TWO REGIMES FOR ONE ENTITY IS CORRECT, NOT A
      DUPLICATE. A Portuguese microentity is `NC-ME` to IES and `M` to SAF-T, and a
      document that files both has said something true that neither says alone. ⛔ A
      receiver must not de-duplicate them, and a validator must not pick one.

      ⚠️ "Portugal" is therefore AT LEAST four frameworks in two codings, not one
      regime, and the same shape recurs wherever a jurisdiction has size tiers.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND IT RECURS EVERYWHERE, WHICH IS WHY `jurisdiction` IS A FIELD AND NOT THE
      KEY. Every jurisdiction met so far tiers its frameworks by entity size:

        Portugal   NCRF · NCRF-PE · NC-ME, beside NIC for adopted IFRS
        Spain      PGC, PGC for SMEs, and a microentity variant beneath it
        Sweden     the K-regelverket: K1 · K2 · K3 · K4, K4 being IFRS

      ⭐ SO THE TIER IS A FACT ABOUT THE ENTITY, AND IT PICKS THE FRAMEWORK. A country
      code cannot: two companies in one country file under different frameworks, and
      a receiver told only "PT" knows nothing about which rules produced the numbers.
      **A jurisdiction is never a regime.**

      ⭐⭐ THE CHART IS NATIONAL TOO, AND THAT IS THE SHARPER HALF. Spain's PGC and
      Sweden's BAS are national charts of accounts; a position code means nothing
      across a border, and `1200` is a different account in each. Any answer naming a
      position must carry the chart that codes it. ⚠️ AND THE AUTHORITY IS NOT ALWAYS
      THE STATE: Sweden's BAS is maintained by an association rather than legislated,
      which is precisely why `taxonomy` is an `anyURI` and not an enumeration of
      governments.

      ⭐ NO REGIME AT ALL IS LEGITIMATE AND COMMON. This is a model of business
      process, not of accounting; a stack of supplies borrowing only operations-
      science terms reports under no financial framework, and saying so by silence
      here is correct because the element is a DECLARATION, not a measurement.

      `id` is a document-local handle. It exists so a conformance profile can name
      which regime it narrows, and so two declarations cannot silently be the same.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="id"           type="xs:token"/>
      <!-- ⛔ A BARE TOKEN ON PURPOSE, AND NOTHING MAY KEY ON IT. `PT`, `PRT` and
           `620` are three authorities' codings of one country and all three
           validate here; this element cannot say which was used, and that is
           accepted rather than overlooked. It is NOT a BorrowedTerm because a
           jurisdiction is never a regime and nothing in this model is allowed to
           depend on it. `framework` carries the authority, and it is required.
           ⚠️ A receiver joining on this field is using it for the one purpose it
           was deliberately made too weak to serve. -->
      <xs:element name="jurisdiction" type="xs:token" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="framework"    type="pm:StatedBorrowedTerm"/>
      <!-- ⛔⛔ A CHART OF ACCOUNTS IS NOT A REPORTING TAXONOMY, AND CONFUSING THEM
           IS THE MISTAKE THIS ELEMENT EXISTS TO CATCH. A chart is the list of ACCOUNTS an
           entity posts to (Spain's PGC, Sweden's BAS, Portugal's SNC código de contas),
           and `1200` is a different account in each. A reporting taxonomy is the list of
           CONCEPTS a filing is tagged with; `http://fasb.org/us-gaap` is one, and it
           belongs to `framework` above. An adopter who files a reporting taxonomy here has
           declared a chart that nobody posts to.

           ⭐ WHAT GOES IN EACH HALF, BECAUSE THE PAIR IS WHAT MAKES THE JOIN WORK.
           `taxonomy` is THE CHART ITSELF, the same URI an `Answer/holds` must carry,
           which is what lets a profile line the two up. `value` is the chart's own
           identifier or edition (`PGC`, `BAS`, `SNC-2016`, the entity's chart name),
           which keeps two editions of one chart distinguishable. The same split
           `framework` uses. ⛔ NOT A POSITION: the position is the answer's half.

           ⭐⭐⭐ A SELF-AUTHORED CHART NAMES THE ENTITY AS ITS OWN TAXONOMY, AND THAT
           CLOSES AN ENTIRE FILING POPULATION. THERE IS NO UNITED STATES CHART OF ACCOUNTS.
           What is published there is a reporting taxonomy; every filer's chart is their
           own and unpublished, so the whole US population has a chart, IN DAILY USE, with
           no publishing authority to borrow from. The filer IS the authority for their own
           chart, and naming themselves satisfies BorrowedTerm honestly rather than evading
           it. ⛔ THE TWO MOVES THIS REPLACES WERE BOTH FALSE: omitting the chart said the
           entity had none, and inventing a taxonomy URI filed an authority that does not
           exist.

           ⚠️ SO `unmeasured` IS NOT THE US ANSWER AND MUST NOT BECOME ITS DUMPING GROUND.
           THE US CHART IS UNPUBLISHED, NOT UNKNOWN, and it is filed as a term. `unmeasured`
           is for a sender who is NOT THE ENTITY (a witness answering about somebody else's
           books) or for a tier not yet assigned where the tier picks the chart, as Spain's
           PGC for SMEs abridges the full PGC.

           ⭐ AND THE CHART IS A SEPARATE AXIS FROM THE FRAMEWORK. Two regimes declaring one
           framework under two authorities' codings share ONE chart: a Portuguese
           microentity is `NC-ME` to IES and `M` to SAF-T, and posts to the SNC chart under
           both.

           ⭐⭐ REQUIRED, MIRRORING `framework`, FOR `StatedRemainder`'s REASON. This type
           asserts a universal: any answer naming a position must carry the chart that
           codes it, and requiring the element forces a regime that has none to SAY SO
           rather than leave a blank a profile cannot tell from an unasked question. A
           non-accounting witness answers `notApplicable` and is right to; `none` is for
           somebody who looked and found there is no chart. The cost is one element per
           regime, and `regime` is ITSELF optional, so it is paid only by a sender who has
           already chosen to declare one.

           ⛔ REJECTED: OPTIONAL AND WRAPPED, which looks like the cheaper half and is the
           incoherent middle. It admits `chart` omitted AND `chart absent reason="none"`:
           two encodings of one fact with no rule saying which to send. That is the mirror
           of the defect `framework`'s wrapper was added to repair. S-1 was two facts
           sharing one encoding; this would be one fact split across two.

           ⛔ REJECTED: ANNOTATION ALONE, left optional and unwrapped. The self-authored
           clause closes the US population but cannot close a sender who is not the entity,
           and it leaves the check this element exists for silent: a receiver cannot tell
           "this regime has no chart" from "nobody said", so an answer holding an unexpected
           chart yields no finding, and no report that it could not ask, either. See
           conformance/README.md. -->
      <xs:element name="chart"        type="pm:StatedBorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="note"         type="xs:string" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Document root.

       ⭐ A DOCUMENT IS A STACK AND, OPTIONALLY, THE OPERATIONS THAT DRAW ON IT. A supply
       with no demand against it is an asset register, which is somebody else's
       schema; requiring the layer means every supply in a document has been asked
       the question "what draws on this?".

       ⭐⭐ THE LAYER REFERENCES ARE VALIDATED. xs:key / xs:keyref are XSD 1.0 and
       work in every stock validator, so a draw naming a layer that does not exist
       is a schema error rather than a convention. A rule with no gate behind it is
       not a rule.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:element name="processModulus">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:element name="notation" type="pm:StatedNotation"/>
        <xs:element name="regime" type="pm:Regime" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
        <xs:element name="stack" type="pm:Stack"/>
        <xs:element name="operation" type="pm:Operation" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      </xs:sequence>
    </xs:complexType>

    <xs:key name="regimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="pm:regime"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>

    <xs:key name="layerName">
      <xs:selector xpath="pm:stack/pm:layer"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:name"/>
    </xs:key>

    <xs:keyref name="drawLayer" refer="pm:layerName">
      <xs:selector xpath="pm:operation/pm:draw"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:layer"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <xs:keyref name="inducesLayer" refer="pm:layerName">
      <xs:selector xpath="pm:operation/pm:induces"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:layer"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <xs:keyref name="couplingFrom" refer="pm:layerName">
      <xs:selector xpath="pm:stack/pm:couplings/pm:coupling"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:from"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <xs:keyref name="couplingTo" refer="pm:layerName">
      <xs:selector xpath="pm:stack/pm:couplings/pm:coupling"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:to"/>
    </xs:keyref>
  </xs:element>

</xs:schema>
